QB.add(38,[{"id":38001,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"A child is born without a thymus. T-cell progenitors are still detected in the bone marrow. Why are mature T cells absent?","o":["T-cell progenitors must migrate to the thymus to differentiate and mature","T cells are made only in the spleen, which also needs the thymus","The thymus produces the haematopoietic stem cells for all T cells","T-cell progenitors arise from the myeloid progenitor that sits inside the thymus itself","T cells mature in the bone marrow but are stored in the thymus"],"e":"T cells emerge in the bone marrow as progenitor cells but migrate to the thymus, where they differentiate and mature into helper or cytotoxic T cells. The T-cell progenitor is still a slightly unspecialised cell and must reach the thymus to function as a T cell. B cells, in contrast, stay in the bone marrow for differentiation.","w":["The spleen is not where T cells are made or mature.","Haematopoietic stem cells are in the bone marrow.","T-cell progenitors come from the lymphoid, not myeloid, progenitor.","T cells mature in the thymus, not the bone marrow."],"k":"T cells arise in the bone marrow and mature in the thymus.","rn":[["","Where do T cell progenitors migrate to differentiate and mature?","Where do T cell progenitors migrate to differentiate and mature?","Thymus"]]},{"id":38002,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts where B and T cells develop?","o":["B cells develop and partially mature in the bone marrow; T cells develop further in the thymus","B cells mature in the thymus; T cells mature in the bone marrow","Both B and T cells mature fully in the thymus","Both B and T cells mature fully in the bone marrow","B cells develop in the lymph nodes, whereas T cells develop in the spleen after birth"],"e":"B cells develop and partially mature in the bone marrow (full immunological maturation occurs in the spleen). T cells originate in the bone marrow but the T-cell progenitor migrates to the thymus to develop further and mature. The lecturer's memory aid: B cells, bone marrow; T cells, thymus.","w":["This reverses the sites.","B cells do not mature in the thymus.","T cells need the thymus to mature.","Lymph nodes and spleen are not the development sites described."],"k":"B cells: bone marrow; T cells: thymus.","rn":[["","B vs T cells: site of development","B vs T cells: site of development","B: bone marrow; T: bone marrow, then thymus"]]},{"id":38003,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"In this lineage diagram, which label marks the T-cell progenitor inside the thymus?","o":["4","3","5","7","8"],"e":"The labels show: 1 haematopoietic stem cell; 2 myeloid progenitor; 3 lymphoid progenitor; 4 T-cell progenitor inside the thymus (green box); 5 T<sub>H</sub> helper cell; 6 T<sub>C</sub> cytotoxic cell; 7 B-cell progenitor; 8 NK cell. The blue centre is bone marrow and the pink sides are peripheral tissues.","w":["3 is the lymphoid progenitor in the bone marrow.","5 is the T helper cell, a product of the progenitor.","7 is the B-cell progenitor outside the thymus.","8 is the natural killer cell."],"k":"Lymphoid progenitor → T-cell progenitor → thymus → T<sub>H</sub> or T<sub>C</sub>.","f":"tc05","rn":[["","Which 2 progenitors does the haematopoietic stem cell (HSC) give rise to?","Which 2 progenitors does the haematopoietic stem cell (HSC) give rise to?","Myeloid progenitor; Lymphoid progenitor"],["","What does the lymphoid progenitor give rise to?","What does the lymphoid progenitor give rise to?","NK cell; T-cell progenitor; B-cell progenitor; Dendritic cell"],["","What does the lymphoid progenitor give rise to on the T cell line?","What does the lymphoid progenitor give rise to on the T cell line?","T cell progenitor"]]},{"id":38004,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"A patient's immune response to a cleared infection fails to switch off, and ongoing inflammation damages healthy tissue. Which T cell class is most likely deficient?","o":["Suppressor T cells","Cytotoxic T cells","Helper T cells","Memory T cells","Naive B cells that have not yet met their antigen"],"e":"Suppressor T cells secrete signalling molecules that bind receptors on other immune cells to terminate their activity, suppressing responses that are no longer needed. The lecturer stressed that powerful immune mechanisms must be switched off at the right time, or inflammation keeps going and our own cells and tissues may be destroyed.","w":["Cytotoxic T cells destroy target cells bearing non-self antigen on MHC class I.","Helper T cells stimulate and coordinate responses rather than terminate them.","Memory T cells persist for a fast secondary response.","B cells are not a T cell class."],"k":"Suppressor T cells terminate responses that are no longer needed.","rn":[["","What is the main job of suppressor T cells?","What is the main job of suppressor T cells?","Terminate other cells' activity; Suppress unneeded responses"],["","Why must suppressor T cells stop responses on time?","Why must suppressor T cells stop responses on time?","Powerful responses must stop; Else tissue destroyed"]]},{"id":38005,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"Which T cell class persists for life in a semi-dormant state and allows the body to bypass the primary immune response on a second infection?","o":["Memory T cells","Suppressor T cells","Helper T cells","Cytotoxic T cells","Progenitor T cells"],"e":"Memory T cells persist for life in a semi-dormant state but are rapidly re-activated on a second exposure to the antigen (pathogen) they are specific for, bypassing the need for the primary immune response on second infection. The lecturer added that T cell memory is an emerging field we do not know much about yet.","w":["Suppressor T cells terminate responses.","Helper T cells secrete cytokines and coordinate.","Cytotoxic T cells kill target cells.","Progenitors are unspecialised precursors, not a functional class."],"k":"Memory T cells: semi-dormant for life, rapidly reactivated.","rn":[["","What do memory T cells bypass on second infection?","What do memory T cells bypass on second infection?","The primary immune response"],["","What happens to memory T cells on second exposure to their antigen?","What happens to memory T cells on second exposure to their antigen?","Rapidly re-activated"],["","How long do memory T cells persist?","How long do memory T cells persist?","For life"]]},{"id":38006,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"The lecturer described one T cell class as the \"most important\" because, without its secretions, immunity basically does not exist. Which class, and what does it secrete?","o":["Helper T cells; cytokines","Cytotoxic T cells; antibodies","Suppressor T cells; histamine","Memory T cells; complement","Helper T cells; antibodies"],"e":"Helper T cells secrete cytokines that bind receptors on B cells and cytotoxic T cells to stimulate their activity, and they coordinate and regulate the adaptive response. The lecturer called them the orchestrators: cytokines trigger responses and allow communication and signalling, so without them immunity basically does not exist.","w":["Cytotoxic T cells kill targets; antibodies are made by B cells.","Suppressor T cells terminate responses; histamine is not their product.","Memory T cells are not described as secreting complement.","Antibodies are made by B cells, not helper T cells."],"k":"Helper T cells: cytokine-secreting orchestrators of immunity.","rn":[["","What do helper T cells secrete?","What do helper T cells secrete?","Cytokines"],["","Does an activated B cell secrete cytokines?","Does an activated B cell secrete cytokines?","No; Only the helper T cell"],["","Helper T cell cytokines bind to receptors on which 2 cells?","Helper T cell cytokines bind to receptors on which 2 cells?","B cells; Cytotoxic T cells"]]},{"id":38007,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which statement about T cell classes compared with B cells is consistent with the lecture?","o":["T cells are split into functional classes, whereas B cell subsets show no major differences","B cells are split into four classes, whereas T cells have none","Both B and T cells have four major functional classes that differ in what they do","Neither B nor T cells have any subsets","T cell classes differ only in their antigen specificity"],"e":"Unlike B cells, where subsets are reported but show no major differences other than antigen specificity, T cells are divided into four functional classes: cytotoxic, helper, suppressor and memory. The major subsets are CD4+ helper and CD8+ cytotoxic T cells.","w":["The lecture divides T cells, not B cells, into four classes.","B cell subsets show no major differences.","B cell subsets are reported but not major.","T cell classes differ in function, not only specificity."],"k":"T cells: four functional classes; B cell subsets differ little.","rn":[["","B vs T cells: subsets","B vs T cells: subsets","B: no major differences; T: CD4+ vs CD8+"]]},{"id":38008,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","a":["cytotoxic t cell","cytotoxic t cells","cytotoxic","killer t cell","ctl","tc","cd8 t cell","cd8+ t cell"],"s":"Name the T cell class that searches for and destroys target cells bearing non-self antigens presented in MHC class I context.","e":"Cytotoxic (killer) T cells search for and destroy target cells that bear non-self antigens presented in MHC class I context. They are CD8+ and are also called CTLs.","k":"Cytotoxic T cells kill cells presenting non-self antigen on MHC I.","rn":[["","What is the main job of cytotoxic T cells?","What is the main job of cytotoxic T cells?","Search and destroy; Cells with non-self antigen; In MHC class I"]]},{"id":38009,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"Why are CD markers used to identify lymphocyte populations?","o":["B and T lymphocytes are virtually impossible to tell apart by microanatomy, so surface proteins are used","CD markers show each lymphocyte's function directly from its number, so CD4 tells you exactly what the cell does in a response","CD markers are directly visible on standard H&amp;E staining of a blood film","CD markers are only found on granulocytes","CD markers are enzymes secreted into the blood"],"e":"In histology, for example with H&amp;E staining, it is virtually impossible to distinguish B and T lymphocytes by microanatomy, though granulocytes (granules) and some phagocytes (nucleus shape) can be recognised. Immunologists therefore identify lymphocytes by the membrane proteins on their surface, named cluster of differentiation (CD).","w":["CD numbers give no clue to function; they reflect order of discovery.","Lymphocytes cannot be told apart on routine H&amp;E microanatomy.","CD markers are used for lymphocytes, not only granulocytes.","CD markers are cell surface molecules."],"k":"Lymphocytes are identified by surface CD proteins, not microanatomy.","rn":[["","Why can lymphocytes not be identified by histology (H&E)?","Why can lymphocytes not be identified by histology (H&E)?","Look virtually identical in microanatomy"],["","What do immunologists use to tell leucocyte sub-types apart?","What do immunologists use to tell leucocyte sub-types apart?","Cell surface (membrane) molecules"]]},{"id":38010,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"A newly discovered surface molecule on lymphocytes is added to the CD list today, after CD371. What does its number tell you?","o":["Only the order in which it was discovered, so it would be CD372","Its function in immune signalling, because CD numbers are grouped according to the job each molecule does on the cell","Which lymphocyte class always expresses it on its cell surface","Its molecular weight in kilodaltons","The chromosome on which its gene is found"],"e":"CD stands for cluster of differentiation, a historical term. The naming gives no clue to each molecule's function: numbers are given chronologically, so CD1 was first, CD2 next, and so on. About 371 are currently used, so a molecule recognised tomorrow would become CD372.","w":["The number gives no clue to function.","The number does not define which class expresses it.","The number is not a molecular weight.","The number is not a chromosome location."],"k":"CD numbers are assigned in order of discovery."},{"id":38011,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"How were CD molecules originally discovered, according to the speaker notes?","o":["By immunising mice with human immune cells and studying the antibodies the mice made","By sequencing the human genome for surface proteins","By staining lymphocytes with H&amp;E and comparing nuclei","By measuring the cytokine levels in the blood of human volunteers over time","By growing T cells in the thymus of mice"],"e":"The speaker notes say the CD family was discovered by immunising mice with human immune cells and studying the antibodies the mice produced against the foreign human antigens. The naming indicates the order of discovery, not function.","w":["Genome sequencing is not the method described.","H&amp;E cannot distinguish lymphocyte types.","Cytokine levels were not the method described.","Growing T cells in mice was not the method described."],"k":"CD molecules were found using mouse antibodies to human immune cells.","rn":[["","How were CD molecules first discovered?","How were CD molecules first discovered?","Mice given human immune cells; Mice's antibodies studied"]]},{"id":38012,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"A flow cytometry report says a T cell is \"CD4+\". What is the most likely identity of the cell?","o":["A T helper cell, because it expresses CD4","A cytotoxic T cell, because it expresses CD4","A B cell, because CD4 marks antibody producers","A suppressor T cell lacking any CD markers","A neutrophil, because CD4 marks granulocytes"],"e":"A cell that is CD4+ expresses CD4 and is therefore likely to be a T helper cell. A CD8+ cell expresses CD8 and is likely to be a cytotoxic T cell. If a cell expresses a CD member it is \"positive\" for it, and the \"+\" is often dropped in common usage.","w":["Cytotoxic T cells are typically CD8+.","CD4 is not described as a B cell marker.","Suppressor T cells are not described as lacking CD markers.","CD4 is not described as a granulocyte marker."],"k":"CD4+ → likely T helper; CD8+ → likely cytotoxic T cell.","rn":[["","What does \"CD4 T cell\" mean without \"+\"?","What does \"CD4 T cell\" mean without \"+\"?","T cell expressing CD4"],["","A CD4+ cell is likely to be which T cell?","A CD4+ cell is likely to be which T cell?","T helper cell"]]},{"id":38013,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"Which statement about CD4 and CD8 on T cells is correct, as explained in the lecture?","o":["A helper T cell may carry some CD8, but CD4 is far more abundant on it","A helper T cell never expresses any CD8 at all","CD4 and CD8 are always present in equal amounts on every T cell in the blood","CD8 marks helper cells and CD4 marks cytotoxic cells","CD4 and CD8 are secreted cytokines"],"e":"The lecturer stressed that CD4+ does not mean a helper cell has no CD8; it means CD4 is more abundant on that cell population. CD4 or CD8 acts as the co-receptor for the T cell receptor, specific to the cell type.","w":["The lecture explicitly says helper cells may have some CD8.","One marker predominates on each class.","CD4 marks helper cells and CD8 marks cytotoxic cells.","CD4 and CD8 are surface co-receptors, not cytokines."],"k":"CD4/CD8 indicate which co-receptor predominates, not absolute absence of the other.","rn":[["","Does a CD4+ helper T cell lack CD8?","Does a CD4+ helper T cell lack CD8?","No; CD4 is more abundant; Co-receptor for its TCR"]]},{"id":38014,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","a":["cluster of differentiation","clusters of differentiation"],"s":"What does the abbreviation CD stand for in \"CD4\" and \"CD8\"?","e":"CD stands for cluster of differentiation, a historical term. CD molecules are cell surface proteins used to distinguish leucocyte sub-types, numbered in order of discovery.","k":"CD = cluster of differentiation.","rn":[["","CD","CD","Cluster of differentiation"]]},{"id":38015,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Why can a T cell receptor not bind a soluble viral protein floating in the blood, whereas a B cell receptor can?","o":["T cell receptors only recognise processed peptides presented by another cell on MHC","T cell receptors are secreted and cannot stay on the cell","T cell receptors bind only carbohydrate antigens","T cell receptors are found only inside the nucleus","T cell receptors need the antigen to be coated with complement proteins before they can bind it"],"e":"A B cell's membrane-bound antibody binds the epitope of an antigen directly. The T cell receptor cannot recognise a soluble antigen or one still attached to a pathogen: the antigen must first be processed and only the relevant epitope presented to it by another cell on a major histocompatibility complex (MHC) molecule. This intermediate step is antigen presentation.","w":["The TCR is always membrane-bound and never soluble.","T cell antigens are always protein peptides.","The TCR is on the cell surface.","Complement coating is not the requirement described."],"k":"TCRs see only processed peptides presented on MHC.","rn":[["","B vs T cells: antigen recognised","B vs T cells: antigen recognised","B: intact protein; T: processed peptides; Presented by another cell"]]},{"id":38016,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which cells are the professional antigen presenting cells able to present on MHC class II?","o":["Dendritic cells, macrophages and B cells","Neutrophils, eosinophils and basophils","All nucleated body cells, including epithelial cells and fibroblasts","Cytotoxic T cells and NK cells","Erythrocytes and platelets"],"e":"Only a select group of immune cells are capable of class II MHC presentation: dendritic cells, macrophages and B cells. Two come from innate immunity (macrophages, dendritic cells) plus B cells. In contrast, the vast majority of nucleated body cells can express class I MHC.","w":["Granulocytes are not listed as professional APCs.","All nucleated cells express class I, not class II.","T cells and NK cells are not professional APCs.","Red cells and platelets are not APCs."],"k":"Professional APCs (MHC II): dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells.","rn":[["","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Only professional APCs; Dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells"],["","What are the 3 professional antigen presenting cells?","What are the 3 professional antigen presenting cells?","Dendritic cells; Macrophages; B cells"]]},{"id":38017,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Dendritic cells are described as having one sole purpose. What is it?","o":["To present antigens to T cells","To produce antibodies","To kill virus-infected cells that display non-self antigen","To secrete histamine","To form the thymus"],"e":"Dendritic cells are white blood cells that emerge from the bone marrow, and their sole purpose is to present antigens to (helper) T cells. They are professional antigen presenting cells and are considered a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity.","w":["Antibodies are produced by B cells.","Killing virus-infected cells is the job of cytotoxic T cells.","Histamine comes from mast cells.","Dendritic cells do not form the thymus."],"k":"Dendritic cells exist to present antigen to T cells.","rn":[["","Which APC has the sole purpose of presenting antigens?","Which APC has the sole purpose of presenting antigens?","Dendritic cells"]]},{"id":38018,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["antigen presentation","antigen presenting","presentation"],"s":"What is the name of the intermediate step in which processed peptide is displayed on MHC so that a T cell receptor can recognise it?","e":"T cell receptors cannot recognise soluble or pathogen-attached antigen directly; the antigen must be processed and presented on MHC by another cell. This intermediate step is called antigen presentation.","k":"Antigen presentation lets TCRs see processed peptides.","rn":[["","Can the TCR recognise soluble or pathogen-attached antigen directly?","Can the TCR recognise soluble or pathogen-attached antigen directly?","No, it must be presented"],["","B vs T cells: antigen recognised","B vs T cells: antigen recognised","B: intact protein; T: processed peptides; Presented by another cell"],["","What intermediate step do T cells need that B cells do not?","What intermediate step do T cells need that B cells do not?","Antigen presentation"]]},{"id":38019,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A respiratory virus enters and replicates inside a bronchial epithelial cell. Which pathway will present its peptides, on which MHC class, and to which T cell?","o":["Endogenous pathway; MHC class I; CD8+ cytotoxic T cell","Exogenous pathway, loaded onto MHC class II and shown to a CD4+ helper T cell in the lymph node","Endogenous pathway; MHC class II; CD4+ helper T cell","Exogenous pathway; MHC class I; CD8+ cytotoxic T cell","No pathway: epithelial cells cannot present antigen"],"e":"The endogenous pathway evolved to deal with intracellular infections, largely viruses: only pathogens inside the cell (in the cytoplasm) are loaded onto MHC class I. The vast majority of nucleated body cells express class I MHC, and class I presentation is recognised by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, which kill the infected cell.","w":["The exogenous pathway handles extracellular antigens in professional APCs.","Class II is the exogenous pathway's MHC.","Class I, not exogenous, is used for cytosolic viral peptides in body cells.","Nearly all nucleated body cells can present on class I."],"k":"Intracellular virus → endogenous → MHC I → CD8+ CTL.","rn":[["","Endogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","Endogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","CD8+ cytotoxic T cell"],["","Endogenous pathway: which MHC class?","Endogenous pathway: which MHC class?","MHC class I"],["","Endogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Endogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Rough ER: bind MHC I; Via Golgi to surface; Shown to CD8+ T cells"]]},{"id":38020,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A macrophage engulfs extracellular bacteria using its pattern recognition receptors. Which pathway presents the bacterial peptides, and to which cells?","o":["Exogenous pathway on MHC class II to CD4+ helper T cells","Endogenous pathway on MHC class I to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells","Exogenous pathway on MHC class I to CD4+ helper T cells","Endogenous pathway on MHC class II to B cells","No pathway, because macrophages cannot present antigen"],"e":"In the exogenous pathway the pathogen is processed from outside: a macrophage's PRRs bind PAMPs on the pathogen, it is engulfed, and elements of it are loaded onto MHC class II. The exogenous pathway evolved to deal with extracellular infections and presents to CD4+ helper T cells.","w":["The endogenous pathway handles cytosolic antigens on class I.","Exogenous peptides go on class II, not class I.","Endogenous antigens use class I, and B cells are not the responding T cell.","Macrophages are professional antigen presenting cells."],"k":"Extracellular pathogen → exogenous → MHC II → CD4+ helper.","rn":[["","Exogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","Exogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","CD4+ helper T cell"],["","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","Captures via surface antibody; Processes (exogenous); Presents on MHC class II"],["","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","MHC class II"]]},{"id":38021,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which statement about class I MHC presentation is correct?","o":["It happens constitutively, so most peptides presented are self-derived and harmless","It happens only during infection","It happens only in dendritic cells and other professional APCs","It presents only extracellular bacterial peptides","It always triggers an immune response"],"e":"The vast majority of vertebrate nucleated body cells can express class I MHC, and cytosolic peptides are presented constitutively (all the time), not just during infection. So most peptides shown in class I context are self-derived and will not trigger an immune response, which is why double-checking by T cells is so important.","w":["Class I presentation is constitutive, not only during infection.","Most nucleated body cells present on class I.","Class I presents cytosolic (endogenous) peptides.","Most class I peptides are self and do not trigger a response."],"k":"Class I presentation is constant and mostly self-peptides.","rn":[["","Most peptides in class I context are derived from what?","Most peptides in class I context are derived from what?","Self"],["","Are cytosolic peptides presented only during infection?","Are cytosolic peptides presented only during infection?","No, constitutively (all the time)"]]},{"id":38022,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which statement correctly compares the two antigen presentation pathways?","o":["Endogenous: nearly all nucleated cells, MHC I; exogenous: professional APCs only, MHC II","Endogenous: professional APCs only, using MHC II; exogenous: all nucleated cells, using MHC I","Both pathways use MHC class I in all cells","Both pathways are found only in dendritic cells","Endogenous evolved for extracellular infection; exogenous for intracellular"],"e":"The endogenous pathway evolved for intracellular infections (largely viruses) and uses MHC class I, expressed by the vast majority of nucleated body cells. The exogenous pathway evolved for extracellular infections, and only a select group of immune cells (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells) can present on class II.","w":["This swaps the two pathways.","The exogenous pathway uses class II.","Endogenous presentation occurs in nearly all nucleated cells.","Endogenous is for intracellular, exogenous for extracellular infection."],"k":"Endogenous = MHC I, all nucleated cells; exogenous = MHC II, APCs.","rn":[["","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Only professional APCs; Dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells"]]},{"id":38023,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Put the steps of the endogenous (class I) pathway in order: (P) peptide binds class I MHC in the RER; (Q) proteasome degrades cytosolic protein; (R) MHC–peptide complex travels via Golgi to the surface; (S) TAP moves peptides into the RER; (T) CD8+ T cell recognises the complex.","o":["Q → S → P → R → T","S → Q → P → R → T","Q → P → S → R → T","Q → S → R → P → T","P → Q → S → R → T"],"e":"Cytosolic proteins are degraded by the proteasome; the peptides associate with the transporter associated with antigen processing (TAP) and are moved into the rough ER; there the peptide binds class I MHC; the complex travels via the Golgi to the cell surface; and it is presented to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells.","w":["The protein must be degraded before TAP can transport peptides.","TAP brings peptides into the RER before they bind MHC I.","Binding to MHC I happens in the RER before Golgi transport.","Peptides must be made and transported before binding MHC I."],"k":"Proteasome → TAP → RER loading on MHC I → Golgi → surface → CD8+.","rn":[["","Endogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Endogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Rough ER: bind MHC I; Via Golgi to surface; Shown to CD8+ T cells"]]},{"id":38024,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A cell line lacks functional TAP. Which step of class I presentation fails?","o":["Transport of cytosolic peptides into the rough ER","Degradation of cytosolic proteins by the proteasome","Removal of CLIP from class II MHC","Phagocytosis of extracellular antigen","Fusion of endosomes with Golgi vesicles"],"e":"In the endogenous pathway, peptides produced by the proteasome associate with the transporter associated with antigen processing (TAP) before translocation into the rough ER, where they meet class I MHC. Without TAP, peptides cannot reach class I MHC, so class I presentation fails.","w":["The proteasome acts before TAP and is unaffected.","CLIP removal is part of the class II pathway.","Phagocytosis is the start of the exogenous pathway.","Endosome–Golgi vesicle fusion is part of the class II pathway."],"k":"TAP moves peptides from cytosol into the RER for MHC I loading.","rn":[["","Are cytosolic peptides presented only during infection?","Are cytosolic peptides presented only during infection?","No, constitutively (all the time)"]]},{"id":38025,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Before it binds a peptide, class I MHC is unstable in the ER. Which molecules chaperone it?","o":["Calnexin, tapasin and β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin","CLIP and the invariant chain","CD4 and CD28","Complement proteins and lysozyme from tears and saliva","Proteasome and TAP"],"e":"Before peptide binding, the class I MHC protein is quite unstable and must be chaperoned by other molecules, for example calnexin, tapasin and β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin (calreticulum is also shown on the figure). Once peptide is bound, the complex is transported via the Golgi to the cell surface.","w":["CLIP and the invariant chain protect class II, not class I.","CD4 and CD28 are T cell surface molecules.","Complement and lysozyme are soluble innate factors.","The proteasome and TAP make and transport peptides, not chaperone MHC I."],"k":"MHC I chaperones: calnexin, tapasin, β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin.","rn":[["","What are the 3 chaperones of class I MHC?","What are the 3 chaperones of class I MHC?","Calnexin; Tapasin; Beta2-microglobulin"]]},{"id":38026,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["proteasome","the proteasome","proteasome complex"],"s":"Name the protein complex that degrades cytosolic proteins into peptides at the start of the endogenous pathway.","e":"In the endogenous pathway, cytosolic proteins are degraded by the proteasome complex. The peptides then associate with TAP and are moved into the rough ER to bind class I MHC.","k":"The proteasome makes peptides for MHC I.","rn":[["","Endogenous pathway: first 2 steps?","Endogenous pathway: first 2 steps?","Proteasome degrades proteins; Peptides join TAP"]]},{"id":38027,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Put the steps of the exogenous (class II) pathway in order: (P) CLIP is degraded and peptide loads onto class II; (Q) antigen is phagocytosed and degraded in endosomes; (R) endosomes fuse with Golgi vesicles carrying class II MHC; (S) class II–peptide is shown to a CD4+ T cell.","o":["Q → R → P → S","R → Q → P → S","Q → P → R → S","P → Q → R → S","Q → R → S → P"],"e":"Extracellular antigens are phagocytosed by professional APCs and degraded in endosomes to peptide fragments. The endosomes fuse with vesicles from the Golgi carrying class II MHC with its binding site protected by CLIP. CLIP is degraded and the extracellular peptides are loaded onto class II MHC, which travels to the surface and presents to the TCR on CD4+ helper T cells.","w":["The antigen must be taken in before vesicle fusion.","CLIP is removed after the vesicles fuse.","Peptide loading cannot precede uptake of the antigen.","Loading must occur before presentation to the T cell."],"k":"Phagocytosis → endosome → fuse with class II vesicle → CLIP removed → load → CD4+.","rn":[["","Exogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Exogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Fuses with MHC II vesicle; CLIP degraded, peptide loaded; Shown to CD4+ T cells"]]},{"id":38028,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"What is the role of CLIP in the class II pathway?","o":["It protects the class II binding site until extracellular peptides are loaded","It moves peptides from the cytosol into the endoplasmic reticulum so that they can reach class II MHC","It stabilises class I MHC before peptide binding","It is the co-receptor that binds class II on T cells","It degrades cytosolic proteins into peptides"],"e":"Class II MHC molecules leave the Golgi in vesicles with their binding site protected by CLIP (class II-associated invariant chain peptide). When these fuse with endosomes containing extracellular antigen fragments, CLIP is proteolytically degraded and the antigen peptides are loaded onto class II MHC.","w":["Moving peptides into the ER is the job of TAP.","Class I is stabilised by calnexin, tapasin and β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin.","CD4 is the co-receptor for class II.","Cytosolic proteins are degraded by the proteasome."],"k":"CLIP guards the MHC II groove until antigen peptide loads.","rn":[["","What is the job of CLIP?","What is the job of CLIP?","Protects MHC II binding site"]]},{"id":38029,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["clip","class ii-associated invariant chain peptide","class ii associated invariant chain peptide","invariant chain"],"s":"Name the peptide that occupies and protects the class II MHC binding site until it is degraded and replaced by antigen peptide.","e":"CLIP, the class II-associated invariant chain peptide, protects the class II binding site as it travels from the Golgi. It is degraded in the endosome so extracellular antigen peptides can load.","k":"CLIP protects the class II binding site.","rn":[["","What is the job of CLIP?","What is the job of CLIP?","Protects MHC II binding site"],["","CLIP","CLIP","Class II-associated invariant chain peptide"]]},{"id":38030,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A helper T cell recognises a foreign peptide presented on class II MHC. What happens next, according to the speaker notes?","o":["Signal transduction makes it secrete chemical messengers that promote responses in other cells","It immediately kills the presenting cell by apoptosis","It secretes antibody against the peptide","It undergoes affinity maturation of its receptor","It moves to the thymus to mature"],"e":"Recognition of the antigen as foreign triggers signal transduction pathways in the helper T cell, which then secretes chemical messengers that promote and maintain immune responses in other cells, such as cytotoxic T cells and B cells. Killing presenting cells is the role of cytotoxic T cells responding to class I.","w":["Killing is done by cytotoxic T cells responding to class I.","Antibody is made by B cells.","T cells do not undergo affinity maturation.","Mature helper cells have already left the thymus."],"k":"Activated helper T cells secrete messengers that drive other immune cells.","rn":[["","What happens when a CD4+ T cell recognises a class II antigen as foreign?","What happens when a CD4+ T cell recognises a class II antigen as foreign?","Signal transduction; Secretes messengers promoting immune responses"]]},{"id":38031,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A dendritic cell takes up a virus from outside, yet the viral peptides end up presented on MHC class I. Which pathway explains this?","o":["The crossover pathway, where exogenous peptides transfer to the class I route","The endogenous pathway, because the virus replicated inside the dendritic cell first","The exogenous pathway, which always loads peptides onto class I in APCs","Negative selection in the thymus, which converts class II peptides to class I","Positive selection in the thymus, which moves peptides between MHC classes"],"e":"The crossover pathway in professional antigen presenting cells lets antigens be taken in through the exogenous route, with the peptide fragments then transferring to the endogenous route for MHC class I presentation. It allows APCs to process viruses without risking becoming infected and destroyed themselves.","w":["In the crossover pathway the APC does not need to be infected; it takes the virus in from outside.","The exogenous pathway normally loads class II, not class I.","Negative selection deletes self-reactive T cells; it does not move peptides.","Positive selection deletes useless T cells; it does not move peptides."],"k":"Crossover: exogenous uptake → class I presentation in APCs.","rn":[["","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","MHC class II"],["","Why is the crossover pathway needed?","Why is the crossover pathway needed?","APCs process viruses; Without being infected"],["","What are the 2 antigen presentation pathways?","What are the 2 antigen presentation pathways?","Endogenous; Exogenous"]]},{"id":38032,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Why is the crossover pathway useful to antigen presenting cells?","o":["They can process viruses and present them on class I without becoming infected themselves","It lets them present their own self peptides on class I so that T cells learn to avoid autoimmunity","It lets them make antibodies directly","It stops CD8+ T cells from ever being activated","It replaces the need for MHC molecules"],"e":"The speaker notes explain that the crossover pathway allows APCs to take in and process viruses without themselves risking becoming infected and subsequently destroyed, because the viral antigens are presented in MHC class I context. It is an important component of a number of cancer immunotherapy and vaccination strategies.","w":["The purpose is presenting viral antigen, not self peptides.","APCs do not make antibodies; B cells do once activated as plasma cells.","Class I presentation activates CD8+ T cells.","MHC molecules are still required."],"k":"Crossover lets APCs present viral antigen on class I safely.","rn":[["","Why is the crossover pathway needed?","Why is the crossover pathway needed?","APCs process viruses; Without being infected"]]},{"id":38033,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which areas of medicine does the lecture link to the crossover pathway?","o":["Cancer immunotherapy and vaccination strategies","Blood transfusion and organ donation","Antibiotic resistance testing and the sterilisation of surgical equipment","Allergy testing and skin prick tests","Genetic screening of newborns"],"e":"The crossover pathway is described in the speaker notes as an important component of a number of cancer immunotherapy and vaccination strategies, because it lets APCs present externally acquired antigen on class I to cytotoxic T cells.","w":["Donor matching is linked to HLA genes, not the crossover pathway.","Antibiotics and sterilisation are not discussed here.","Allergy testing is not linked to the crossover pathway.","Newborn screening is not mentioned."],"k":"Crossover pathway underpins some cancer immunotherapy and vaccine strategies.","rn":[["","The crossover pathway is used in which 2 strategies?","The crossover pathway is used in which 2 strategies?","Cancer immunotherapy; Vaccination"]]},{"id":38034,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"In this figure, which label marks the transporter that moves peptides into the rough ER?","o":["3","2","4","8","9"],"e":"The labels show: 1 endogenous antigen in the cytosol; 2 the proteasome; 3 TAP moving peptides into the RER; 4 class I MHC with chaperones; 5 peptide loaded on class I; 6 the Golgi complex; 7 exogenous antigen; 8 CLIP on class II MHC; 9 class II MHC with invariant chain; 10 class I on the surface; 11 class II on the surface. Left of the dashed line is the endogenous pathway; right is exogenous.","w":["2 is the proteasome.","4 is class I MHC with its chaperones.","8 is CLIP on class II.","9 is class II MHC with its invariant chain."],"k":"TAP carries cytosolic peptides into the RER for MHC I.","f":"tc09","rn":[["","Are cytosolic peptides presented only during infection?","Are cytosolic peptides presented only during infection?","No, constitutively (all the time)"]]},{"id":38035,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"In the same figure, what distinguishes the left half from the right half?","o":["Left: endogenous pathway, class I, somatic cells; right: exogenous pathway, class II, APCs","Left: exogenous pathway, class II; right: endogenous pathway, class I","Left: B cell pathway; right: T cell pathway","Left: innate pathway; right: adaptive pathway","Both halves show the same class II pathway"],"e":"Left of the dashed line (blue arrows) is the endogenous pathway, using class I MHC in every somatic cell. Right (pink arrows) is the exogenous pathway, using class II MHC in antigen presenting cells only. Both end at the cell surface.","w":["This swaps the two halves.","The halves are pathways, not B and T cells.","Both halves are antigen processing pathways, not innate vs adaptive.","The two halves show different pathways."],"k":"Endogenous/class I/somatic cells vs exogenous/class II/APCs.","f":"tc09","rn":[["","What are the 2 antigen presentation pathways?","What are the 2 antigen presentation pathways?","Endogenous; Exogenous"],["","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","MHC class II"],["","Can all somatic cells present antigen?","Can all somatic cells present antigen?","Yes; Endogenous (MHC I)"]]},{"id":38036,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which statement about the MHC and HLA is correct?","o":["The HLA gene complex encodes the MHC molecules found in somatic and antigen presenting cells","MHC molecules encode the HLA genes that are found only in the cells of the thymus","HLA molecules are antibodies made by B cells","MHC is found only on red blood cells","HLA genes encode the T cell receptor"],"e":"The HLA (human leucocyte antigen) gene complex in humans encodes the major histocompatibility complex molecules found in somatic or antigen presenting cells. MHC class I and class II complexes show diversity in the antigen binding site, with a degree of polymorphism between molecules.","w":["HLA genes encode MHC molecules, not the reverse.","HLA molecules are not antibodies.","MHC is found on somatic and antigen presenting cells.","The TCR is encoded by rearranged α and β gene segments, not HLA genes."],"k":"HLA genes encode MHC molecules.","rn":[["","Which genes encode the MHC molecules in humans?","Which genes encode the MHC molecules in humans?","HLA gene complex"]]},{"id":38037,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A transplant team searches for a kidney donor and tries to match the class I HLA regions as closely as possible. Which regions do they focus on, and why?","o":["B, C and A, because class I presents self peptides that T cells must not react to","DP, DQ and DR, because class II MHC is found on every nucleated cell of the donor kidney","B, C and A, because they encode antibody heavy chains","DP, DQ and DR, because they encode the T cell receptor","Only the region encoding CLIP, because it controls tolerance"],"e":"The lecturer explained that class I in each cell presents self (cytosolic) peptides, and we do not want T cells to react to that. So when looking for donors, we look for as close a match as possible in the class I HLA regions B, C and A. The class II regions are DP, DQ and DR.","w":["DP, DQ and DR are class II regions; class II is on professional APCs, not every cell.","HLA genes encode MHC, not antibody chains.","HLA genes do not encode the TCR.","CLIP is not an HLA region."],"k":"Donor matching targets class I HLA-B, -C, -A.","rn":[["","Why is HLA important in donor matching?","Why is HLA important in donor matching?","Class I presents self peptides; T cells must not react"]]},{"id":38038,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which statement about the capacity of the MHC binding groove is consistent with the lecture?","o":["MHC class I usually binds a small peptide; class II has a slightly higher capacity","MHC class I binds whole intact proteins, whereas class II binds only single amino acids","Both classes bind only carbohydrate antigens","Class II cannot bind peptides at all","Class I binds larger peptides than class II"],"e":"The antigen binds in the peptide-binding groove. The lecturer noted that MHC class I usually allows a small peptide to bind, while class II has a slightly higher capacity. MHC class I and II complexes show diversity in the antigen binding site (polymorphism).","w":["Class I binds small peptides, not whole proteins.","T cell antigens are protein peptides, not carbohydrates.","Class II binds peptides from the exogenous pathway.","Class II has the slightly higher capacity."],"k":"MHC I: small peptides; MHC II: slightly larger capacity.","rn":[["","MHC class I vs II: peptide capacity","MHC class I vs II: peptide capacity","Class I: small peptide; Class II: slightly higher"]]},{"id":38039,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"In this figure of the MHC molecules, what is labelled 2?","o":["β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin, part of the class I molecule","The peptide-binding groove formed between the domains of class II","The cell membrane","The class II β1 domain","The HLA-A gene product"],"e":"The labels show: 1 the class I molecule, whose α1 and α2 domains form the peptide-binding groove; 2 β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin, part of class I; 3 the α3 domain anchored in the membrane; 4 the class II molecule (α1 + β1 groove, α2 and β2 below); 5 the cell membrane; 6 the human HLA complex table. Class I has one membrane-spanning chain plus β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin; class II has two membrane chains.","w":["The class II groove is formed by α1 and β1 at label 4.","The membrane is label 5.","β1 is part of the class II molecule at label 4.","HLA-A appears in the table at label 6."],"k":"Class I = α chain + β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin; class II = α + β chains.","f":"tc10","rn":[["","What are the 3 chaperones of class I MHC?","What are the 3 chaperones of class I MHC?","Calnexin; Tapasin; Beta2-microglobulin"]]},{"id":38040,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Based on the structures in this figure, which statement correctly contrasts class I and class II MHC?","o":["Class I has one membrane-spanning chain plus β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin; class II has two membrane-spanning chains","Class I has two membrane-spanning chains; class II has one","Neither class is anchored in the membrane","Class II contains β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin and class I does not","Both are single chains with no peptide groove"],"e":"The class I molecule has an α chain (α1, α2, α3), with α1 and α2 forming the peptide-binding groove and α3 in the membrane, plus β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin. The class II molecule has two chains, α and β, both spanning the membrane, with α1 and β1 forming its groove.","w":["This reverses the chain arrangement.","Both classes are anchored in the membrane.","β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin belongs to class I.","Both have a peptide-binding groove."],"k":"MHC I: α chain + β<sub>2</sub>m; MHC II: α + β chains.","f":"tc10","rn":[["","CLIP","CLIP","Class II-associated invariant chain peptide"]]},{"id":38041,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["hla","human leucocyte antigen","human leukocyte antigen","hla genes"],"s":"Name the human gene complex that encodes the major histocompatibility complex molecules.","e":"The HLA (human leucocyte antigen) gene complex encodes the MHC molecules. Class II regions are DP, DQ and DR; class I regions are B, C and A.","k":"HLA genes encode MHC.","rn":[["","Which genes encode the MHC molecules in humans?","Which genes encode the MHC molecules in humans?","HLA gene complex"],["","Which HLA regions encode MHC class II?","Which HLA regions encode MHC class II?","DP; DQ; DR"]]},{"id":38042,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A cytotoxic T cell's TCR binds a viral peptide on an infected cell. Before killing, what does its CD8 molecule check?","o":["That the MHC class I molecule presenting the peptide is from a matched allele","That the peptide is bound to class II MHC","That the infected cell has already secreted cytokines into the surrounding tissue","That antibodies are already attached to the virus","That the cell is a professional antigen presenting cell"],"e":"Once peptide is loaded into MHC class I and presented on the infected cell, it engages the TCR on a passing CD8+ cytotoxic T cell (CTL). The CD8 molecule simultaneously \"double checks\" that the peptide is presented correctly by recognising that the MHC class I molecule is from a matched allele. The CTL only kills once these engagements are complete.","w":["CTLs respond to class I, not class II.","The check concerns MHC I, not cytokine secretion by the target.","Antibody binding is not the CD8 check.","CTLs respond to infected body cells, not only professional APCs."],"k":"CD8 double-checks the MHC class I allele before the CTL kills.","rn":[["","What does CD8 double check?","What does CD8 double check?","MHC I is matched allele; Peptide presented correctly"]]},{"id":38043,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Why is a double check by CD8 particularly important for cytotoxic T cells?","o":["Class I constantly presents self peptides, so the CTL must avoid killing healthy cells","Class I presents only viral peptides, so in fact no double check is ever needed by the CTL","CD8 is needed to secrete antibody before killing","Class I is only found on APCs, which must be protected","The check converts class I peptides into class II"],"e":"Class I MHC presents cytosolic peptides constitutively, so most peptides shown are self-derived. The lecturer stressed that T cells must check the complex, confirm the peptide is not one from our own body, and not trigger a response to self. Only then does the CTL kill the virally infected cell.","w":["Class I presents mostly self peptides.","CTLs do not secrete antibody.","Class I is expressed by nearly all nucleated cells.","The check does not convert peptides between classes."],"k":"Constant self-peptide display on MHC I makes the CD8 check essential.","rn":[["","Why is HLA important in donor matching?","Why is HLA important in donor matching?","Class I presents self peptides; T cells must not react"]]},{"id":38044,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"In this figure, which label marks the co-receptor that checks the MHC class I molecule?","o":["6","5","7","8","9"],"e":"The labels show: 1 viral gene in the infected cell; 2 viral protein; 3 peptide fragments; 4 a post-Golgi vesicle carrying peptide on MHC class I; 5 MHC class I with peptide on the cell surface; 6 CD8 checking the MHC class I; 7 the T-cell receptor binding the peptide; 8 activation of the cytotoxic T cell; 9 killing of the infected cell.","w":["5 is MHC class I with its peptide.","7 is the T-cell receptor.","8 marks activation of the CTL.","9 marks the kill."],"k":"CTL recognition: TCR binds peptide; CD8 checks MHC I.","f":"tc11","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of CTL activation?","What are the 3 steps of CTL activation?","Peptide in MHC I; Engages CD8+ TCR; CD8 double checks MHC I"]]},{"id":38045,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"How does an activated cytotoxic T cell destroy a virally infected cell, according to the lecture?","o":["It triggers apoptosis and damages the cell membrane, including releasing enzymes","It secretes antibodies that neutralise the virus","It phagocytoses the infected cell","It presents the virus on MHC class II to nearby B cells so they make antibody","It releases histamine onto the cell"],"e":"On activation, CD8+ T cells engage with and attack target cells by triggering apoptosis and damaging the cell membrane. The lecturer added that they release enzymes that help destroy the cell and also bind it directly, triggering apoptosis, to create a hostile environment for the replicating virus.","w":["Antibodies are made by B cells.","Phagocytosis is the job of phagocytes such as macrophages.","CTLs do not present antigen to B cells.","Histamine comes from mast cells."],"k":"CTLs kill by apoptosis and membrane damage.","rn":[["","What 2 ways do CTLs kill infected cells?","What 2 ways do CTLs kill infected cells?","Release damaging enzymes; Bind to trigger apoptosis"]]},{"id":38046,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A helper T cell meets its peptide on MHC class II on a dendritic cell. Which co-receptor interaction from the lecture is part of the double check?","o":["CD28 on the T cell binding B7 on the APC","CD8 binding class I MHC","CD40 on the antigen presenting cell binding a circulating antibody molecule","TAP binding CLIP on the T cell","β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin binding CD4"],"e":"For class II presentation, a passing T helper cell binds the MHC class II complex, analyses the antigen and also runs double checks through CD28 (binding B7 on the APC, as shown in stage 1 of the figure). It then becomes activated and secretes cytokines that trigger an immune response.","w":["CD8 and class I belong to cytotoxic T cell recognition.","CD40 is on the B cell and binds CD40L on the T cell.","TAP and CLIP are processing molecules inside the APC.","β<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin is part of class I MHC."],"k":"Th priming: TCR–MHC II plus CD28–B7 double check.","rn":[["","Which receptor does the helper T cell use to double check the APC?","Which receptor does the helper T cell use to double check the APC?","CD28"],["","Which molecule pairs act between APC, Th and B cell?","Which molecule pairs act between APC, Th and B cell?","B7 and CD28; CD40L and CD40"]]},{"id":38047,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A helper T cell recognises antigen presented by a B cell rather than a dendritic cell. What is the specific result?","o":["It secretes soluble factors that make the B cell divide and secrete antibody","It becomes alert but releases nothing","It kills the B cell by apoptosis","It turns the B cell into a memory T cell that persists for life in the tissues","It stops the B cell from presenting antigen again"],"e":"If the presenting cell is a professional APC, the helper cell becomes \"alert\" that its TCR may be needed, and signalling changes its behaviour. If it recognises antigen presented by a B cell, signal transduction leads to secretion of soluble factors that stimulate the B cell to divide and secrete soluble antibody.","w":["Becoming alert describes presentation by a professional APC.","Helper T cells do not kill B cells.","B cells do not become T cells.","The interaction activates the B cell."],"k":"Th recognising B cell antigen → B cell division and antibody secretion.","rn":[["","Helper T recognises antigen on a B cell: then?","Helper T recognises antigen on a B cell: then?","T cell secretes soluble factors; B cell divides, secretes antibody"]]},{"id":38048,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"What would happen to the B cell/antibody arm of immunity without the helper T cell double-check?","o":["B cells would never become fully activated, so the antibody response would not take place","B cells would become overactive and attack self","B cells would switch to producing T cell receptors","Nothing would change, because B cells are fully independent of T cells for activation","B cells would present antigen on class I instead"],"e":"The speaker notes state that without this double-checking process B cells will never become fully activated, and the B cell/antibody arm of the immune response will not take place. B cells need both antigen binding and T cell help (growth factors).","w":["The outcome is failure of activation, not autoimmunity.","B cells do not make T cell receptors.","B cells depend on helper T cells for full activation.","B cells present on class II to helper T cells."],"k":"No Th double-check → no full B cell activation.","rn":[["","What happens to B cells without helper T cell double checking?","What happens to B cells without helper T cell double checking?","Never fully activated; No antibody arm of response"]]},{"id":38049,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"In this figure, what interaction is labelled 7 in stage 2?","o":["CD40L on the Th cell binding CD40 on the B cell","B7 on the APC binding CD28 on the Th cell","The T-dependent antigen with B and T cell epitopes","The APC processing the antigen","MHC II presenting peptide to the Th cell in stage 1"],"e":"The figure shows stage 1, priming of the Th cell (T-dependent antigen with a B cell epitope and a T cell epitope; APC processing; B7–CD28; MHC II), and stage 2, activation of the B cell by the primed Th cell after it recognises processed antigen. In stage 2, CD40L on the Th cell binds CD40 on the B cell, leading to B cell activation.","w":["B7–CD28 is in stage 1.","The antigen is at label 2.","APC processing is at label 3.","MHC II presentation in stage 1 is at label 5."],"k":"Stage 2: CD40L (Th) – CD40 (B cell) → B cell activation.","f":"tc12","rn":[["","Which molecule pairs act between APC, Th and B cell?","Which molecule pairs act between APC, Th and B cell?","B7 and CD28; CD40L and CD40"]]},{"id":38050,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"In stage 2 of this figure, how does the B cell obtain the antigen it presents to the primed Th cell?","o":["It captures the antigen with its surface antibody, processes it and shows it on MHC II","It receives the processed peptide directly from the dendritic cell that primed the Th cell","It makes the antigen itself from its own DNA","It takes the peptide from the Th cell's TCR","It gets the antigen through TAP from the cytosol"],"e":"The lecturer explained that a B cell captures an antigen via the antibody on its surface (Ag capture), processes it by the exogenous pathway and presents it on MHC class II to a helper T cell. Recognition by the primed Th cell then also triggers activation of the B cell.","w":["The B cell captures and processes antigen itself.","B cells do not make the antigen.","TCRs do not hand peptides to B cells.","TAP is part of the class I (endogenous) pathway."],"k":"B cells capture antigen via surface antibody and present it on MHC II.","f":"tc12","rn":[["","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","Captures via surface antibody; Processes (exogenous); Presents on MHC class II"]]},{"id":38051,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"During the lecture, a student asked whether the activated B cell releases cytokines in the Th–B cell interaction. Which answer is consistent with the lecture?","o":["No; only the T helper cell secretes cytokines in this interaction","Yes; the B cell is the main source of cytokines","Yes; both the B cell and the T helper cell secrete identical cytokines at the same time","No; neither cell secretes anything","Only memory B cells secrete cytokines"],"e":"In this interaction only the T helper cell secretes cytokines; the B cell has receptors on its surface that bind ligands on the T cell. The B cell is not simply a helper to the T cell: it can also be activated at the same time and is the cell that goes on to differentiate into memory cells.","w":["The B cell is not described as the cytokine source.","Only the T helper cell secretes cytokines here.","The T helper cell does secrete cytokines.","Memory B cells are not described as cytokine sources."],"k":"In Th–B interaction, cytokines come from the T helper cell.","rn":[["","Does an activated B cell secrete cytokines?","Does an activated B cell secrete cytokines?","No; Only the helper T cell"],["","Helper T cell cytokines bind to receptors on which 2 cells?","Helper T cell cytokines bind to receptors on which 2 cells?","B cells; Cytotoxic T cells"],["","What do helper T cells secrete?","What do helper T cells secrete?","Cytokines"]]},{"id":38052,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["cd28"],"s":"Name the receptor on the T helper cell that binds B7 on the antigen presenting cell as part of the double check during priming.","e":"During priming of the Th cell, B7 on the APC binds CD28 on the Th cell. The lecturer described this as a double check that the epitope on MHC class II is not a self cytosolic peptide before activation.","k":"CD28 (Th) binds B7 (APC).","rn":[["","Which receptor does the helper T cell use to double check the APC?","Which receptor does the helper T cell use to double check the APC?","CD28"],["","Which molecule pairs act between APC, Th and B cell?","Which molecule pairs act between APC, Th and B cell?","B7 and CD28; CD40L and CD40"],["","What do CD28 and CD40 check?","What do CD28 and CD40 check?","Epitope is not self; (Not cytosolic peptide)"]]},{"id":38053,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"What are the CD28 and CD40 \"arms\" for, according to the lecturer's answer to a student?","o":["Double checking that the epitope on MHC II is foreign rather than a self cytosolic peptide","Transporting peptides from the cytosol into the endoplasmic reticulum for loading onto MHC","Killing the presenting cell after recognition","Producing antibodies against the antigen","Rearranging the T cell receptor genes"],"e":"When asked about CD28 and CD40, the lecturer said they are for double checking: they check that the epitope loaded in MHC class II is not a cytosolic (self) peptide, making sure it is foreign before the cells are activated.","w":["Peptide transport into the ER is done by TAP.","Killing is the role of cytotoxic T cells.","Antibody production belongs to B cells.","TCR gene rearrangement occurs during development."],"k":"CD28/CD40 interactions double-check that the antigen is non-self.","rn":[["","What do CD28 and CD40 check?","What do CD28 and CD40 check?","Epitope is not self; (Not cytosolic peptide)"]]},{"id":38054,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A student asks whether antigen presentation to T cells is the start of the immune response. Which answer fits the lecture?","o":["No; innate immunity starts the response and adaptive immunity comes into play later","Yes; T cells always act before any innate cell, because antigen presentation is the very first step of every response","Yes; innate immunity only begins after T cells are activated","No; antibodies are always made before any immune cell acts","No; the thymus starts every immune response"],"e":"The lecturer explained that the immune response starts with innate immunity, and adaptive immunity comes into play later. T cell antigen presentation is part of the adaptive response and is how the immune system moves towards immunological memory.","w":["Innate immunity acts first.","Innate immunity does not wait for T cells.","Antibodies come from activated B cells later in the response.","The thymus is where T cells mature, not the trigger of responses."],"k":"Innate first; adaptive (T cells) later, leading to memory.","rn":[["","Which cells are seen as a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity?","Which cells are seen as a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity?","Dendritic cells (APCs)"],["","Are innate and adaptive immunity separate?","Are innate and adaptive immunity separate?","No, they communicate"],["","T cells are an essential part of which immune response?","T cells are an essential part of which immune response?","Adaptive immune response"]]},{"id":38055,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"A CD4+ T cell and a CD8+ T cell both meet the same infected tissue. Which pairing correctly matches each cell to what it recognises?","o":["CD4+: MHC II on professional APCs; CD8+: MHC I on infected body cells","CD4+: MHC I on infected body cells; CD8+: MHC II on professional antigen presenting cells","Both recognise MHC I on any cell","Both recognise soluble antigen in the blood","CD4+: antibodies on B cells; CD8+: complement"],"e":"CD4+ T cells recognise antigens presented in MHC class II context by professional APCs. CD8+ T cells recognise antigens presented in MHC class I context on infected body cells. CD4 and CD8 act as TCR co-receptors in these MHC interactions.","w":["This swaps the classes.","CD4+ cells recognise MHC II.","T cells cannot recognise soluble antigen directly.","T cells recognise peptide–MHC, not antibodies or complement."],"k":"CD4 ↔ MHC II (APCs); CD8 ↔ MHC I (infected cells).","rn":[["","CD4+ vs CD8+: presenting cell","CD4+ vs CD8+: presenting cell","CD4+: professional APCs; CD8+: infected body cells"]]},{"id":38056,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which co-receptor and MHC class pairing is a useful way to remember T cell recognition?","o":["CD8 pairs with MHC I and CD4 with MHC II; both products equal 8","CD8 pairs with MHC II and CD4 pairs with MHC I, so both products still equal 8","CD4 and CD8 both pair only with MHC I","CD4 and CD8 both pair only with MHC II","Neither CD4 nor CD8 interacts with MHC"],"e":"CD8 acts as the co-receptor for MHC class I interactions (cytotoxic T cells), and CD4 for MHC class II interactions (helper T cells). A simple rule: 8 × 1 = 8 and 4 × 2 = 8. Full signal transduction also involves additional co-receptor molecules.","w":["The pairings are reversed.","CD4 pairs with MHC II.","CD8 pairs with MHC I.","CD4 and CD8 are the co-receptors in MHC interactions."],"k":"CD8–MHC I, CD4–MHC II (\"rule of 8\").","rn":[["","CD4+ vs CD8+: MHC class recognised","CD4+ vs CD8+: MHC class recognised","CD4+: MHC class II; CD8+: MHC class I"]]},{"id":38057,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A drug blocks the proteasome in all body cells. Which T cell response would be most directly impaired?","o":["CD8+ T cell recognition of viral peptides on MHC class I","CD4+ T cell recognition of bacterial peptides on MHC class II","B cell recognition of intact soluble antigen","Innate recognition of PAMPs by PRRs","NK cell binding to antibody Fc domains"],"e":"The proteasome degrades cytosolic proteins in the endogenous pathway, producing the peptides that TAP moves into the ER for class I MHC. Blocking it would reduce class I presentation of intracellular (e.g. viral) peptides to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells. Class II presentation uses endosomal degradation of extracellular antigen.","w":["Class II peptides come from endosomes in the exogenous pathway.","B cells recognise intact antigen without MHC processing.","PRR recognition of PAMPs does not need the proteasome.","NK binding to antibody Fc does not require proteasome processing."],"k":"Proteasome block → less MHC I presentation to CD8+ cells.","rn":[["","CD4+ vs CD8+: MHC class recognised","CD4+ vs CD8+: MHC class recognised","CD4+: MHC class II; CD8+: MHC class I"],["","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","Captures via surface antibody; Processes (exogenous); Presents on MHC class II"],["","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Only professional APCs; Dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells"]]},{"id":38058,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A professional APC cannot degrade CLIP. Which consequence follows?","o":["Extracellular peptides cannot be loaded onto class II, so CD4+ T cells are not activated","Cytosolic peptides cannot reach class I, so CD8+ T cells are not activated","B cells cannot secrete antibody directly from the APC","The APC cannot phagocytose bacteria at all","Class I MHC becomes unstable in the ER"],"e":"In the exogenous pathway CLIP protects the class II binding site until it is proteolytically degraded and extracellular peptides are loaded. If CLIP stays bound, extracellular peptides cannot load onto class II, so presentation to CD4+ helper T cells fails.","w":["Class I loading depends on TAP, not CLIP.","B cells secrete antibody themselves; APCs do not.","CLIP acts after phagocytosis, which is unaffected.","Class I stability depends on its chaperones, not CLIP."],"k":"CLIP removal is needed for class II loading and CD4+ activation.","rn":[["","Exogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Exogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Fuses with MHC II vesicle; CLIP degraded, peptide loaded; Shown to CD4+ T cells"]]},{"id":38059,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which statement about which cells express each MHC class is correct?","o":["Class I: the vast majority of nucleated body cells; class II: dendritic cells, macrophages and B cells","Class I: only dendritic cells; class II: all nucleated cells","Both classes are expressed only by T cells","Class II: all nucleated cells; class I: only B cells","Neither class is found on B cells"],"e":"The vast majority of vertebrate nucleated body cells can express class I MHC. Only a select group of immune cells can present on class II: dendritic cells, macrophages and B cells, the professional antigen presenting cells.","w":["This reverses the distribution.","MHC is expressed by presenting cells, not T cells.","Class II is restricted to professional APCs.","B cells present on class II."],"k":"MHC I on most nucleated cells; MHC II on professional APCs.","rn":[["","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Only professional APCs; Dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells"],["","Which cells express class I MHC?","Which cells express class I MHC?","Most nucleated cells"]]},{"id":38060,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["b7"],"s":"Name the molecule on the antigen presenting cell that binds CD28 during priming of the helper T cell.","e":"In stage 1 of the priming figure, B7 on the antigen presenting cell binds CD28 on the T helper cell, alongside TCR recognition of peptide on MHC II.","k":"B7 (APC) binds CD28 (Th).","rn":[["","Which molecule pairs act between APC, Th and B cell?","Which molecule pairs act between APC, Th and B cell?","B7 and CD28; CD40L and CD40"]]},{"id":38061,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"Why must T cell receptor diversity be generated by gene rearrangement rather than inherited directly?","o":["The number of antigen shapes is too large to encode in the germline","The germline encodes only one TCR, which is copied into every T cell unchanged","T cells receive their receptor genes from B cells in the bone marrow","TCRs are made from pathogen genes taken up during infection","The thymus synthesises receptors without needing any genes at all"],"e":"The number of potential antigen shapes that T cell receptors must recognise is huge, and it would not be possible for all of this to be encoded in the germline (genome). So, as in B cells, gene segments are rearranged to create variety in the receptors.","w":["Diversity comes from rearrangement, not one copied receptor.","T cells rearrange their own TCR genes; they do not take genes from B cells.","Receptors are made from host gene segments, not pathogen genes.","TCRs are encoded by rearranged gene segments."],"k":"Too many antigen shapes for the germline → TCR gene rearrangement.","rn":[["","Why are TCR specificities not germline-encoded?","Why are TCR specificities not germline-encoded?","Huge number of shapes"]]},{"id":38062,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"Which description of the αβ T cell receptor is correct?","o":["A heterodimer of α and β subunits, each with a variable binding domain and a membrane constant domain","A homodimer of two identical heavy chains joined to two light chains, like an antibody molecule","A single β chain with no constant region at all","A pentamer of five units joined by a J chain","A soluble protein secreted into the blood"],"e":"T cell receptors include an alpha and a beta subunit, making the receptor a heterodimer (αβTCR). They contain a variable antigen-binding domain and a cell membrane-associated constant domain. The lecturer called it simpler than the BCR, which has heavy and light chains.","w":["Heavy and light chains belong to antibodies and BCRs.","The TCR has both α and β chains, each with constant regions.","Pentamers with J chains describe IgM.","The TCR is never soluble."],"k":"TCR = αβ heterodimer; variable binding domain + membrane constant domain.","rn":[["","What are the 2 domains of the TCR?","What are the 2 domains of the TCR?","Variable antigen binding domain; Membrane associated constant domain"]]},{"id":38063,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"Which gene segments are combined to make each TCR subunit?","o":["α: V, J, C; β: V, D, J, C","α: V, D, J, C; β: V, J, C","Both α and β: V and C only","α: D and J only; β: V and C only","Both: the same single gene inherited unchanged"],"e":"The subunits are each made by randomly combining V/J/C (alpha) or V/D/J/C (beta) genomic regions. The largest diversity is in the V regions, and all three mRNA \"frames\" can be used during translation. This mirrors the light (VJC) and heavy (VDJC) chains of antibodies.","w":["This swaps the segments: β, not α, has the D segment.","Both subunits also use J segments.","Both subunits use V, J and C.","The genes are rearranged, not inherited unchanged."],"k":"TCR α = VJC; TCR β = VDJC."},{"id":38064,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"Where is the greatest diversity in the T cell receptor generated?","o":["In the V regions","In the constant domains, which differ between every T cell","In the CD4 and CD8 co-receptors attached to the TCR","In the MHC molecules that the TCR is made from","In the J chain that links TCR units together"],"e":"The largest diversity is in the V regions of the α and β subunits, and all three mRNA frames can be used during translation, adding further variety. The constant domain is membrane-associated and does not vary to bind antigen.","w":["The constant domain anchors the receptor and is not the main source of diversity.","CD4 and CD8 are co-receptors, not the variable binding domain.","MHC molecules are on presenting cells and are not part of the TCR.","The J chain is part of IgM and IgA, not the TCR."],"k":"TCR diversity is greatest in the V regions.","rn":[["","Where is the largest diversity in TCR genes?","Where is the largest diversity in TCR genes?","V regions"]]},{"id":38065,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which of the following is TRUE of T cells but NOT of B cells?","o":["No affinity maturation and no C-domain substitution during a response","They mutate their receptor to improve affinity during the immune response","They secrete a soluble form of their receptor into the blood plasma","They switch class from IgM to IgG during a secondary response","They recognise intact protein antigens without needing presentation"],"e":"Unlike B cells, there is no affinity maturation in T cells, nor is there substitution of C domains (class switching) during the immune response; T cells have no classes to switch. B cells mutate their receptor in affinity maturation, secrete soluble antibody, class switch and recognise intact antigen.","w":["Affinity maturation is a B cell feature.","Secreting a soluble receptor is a B cell feature.","Class switching is a B cell feature.","Recognising intact antigen is a B cell feature."],"k":"T cells: no affinity maturation, no class switching.","rn":[["","B cells vs T cells: affinity maturation","B cells vs T cells: affinity maturation","B: mutate receptor; T: none"]]},{"id":38066,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"A researcher searches patient serum for free T cell receptors after an infection. What should they expect?","o":["None: the TCR always remains membrane-bound","High levels, because activated T cells secrete their receptors as antibodies","Only TCRs of the IgG class after class switching","TCRs bound to complement in the circulation","TCRs released only from memory T cells"],"e":"The lecturer stressed that you will never see the T cell receptor in a soluble form. B cell antibodies can be released as soluble antibodies once the cell has matured, but in T cells the receptor always remains membrane-bound: it is on the cell surface only.","w":["T cells do not secrete their receptors.","TCRs have no classes such as IgG.","TCRs are not free in the circulation.","Memory T cells also keep the receptor on the surface."],"k":"TCRs are never soluble.","rn":[["","Is the TCR ever found in soluble form?","Is the TCR ever found in soluble form?","No, always membrane bound"],["","B vs T cell: can the receptor be soluble?","B vs T cell: can the receptor be soluble?","B: yes (antibody); T: never, membrane bound"]]},{"id":38067,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"Which statement about the TCR repertoire compared with B cells is consistent with the speaker notes?","o":["The potential repertoire of TCR shapes exceeds that available to B cells","The TCR repertoire is much smaller because T cells have no gene rearrangement","The TCR repertoire is identical in every T cell in the body","The TCR repertoire is limited to the germline-encoded PRRs","T cells borrow their receptor repertoire from antibodies"],"e":"TCRs show increased antigen-binding diversity similarly to the BCR, through gene recombination. The speaker notes add that the potential repertoire of different TCR shapes exceeds that available to B cells.","w":["T cells do rearrange their receptor genes.","Each T cell has its own receptor.","PRRs are innate, germline-encoded receptors.","T cells make their own receptors."],"k":"TCR repertoire exceeds the B cell repertoire.","rn":[["","How does TCR repertoire size compare with B cells?","How does TCR repertoire size compare with B cells?","Exceeds B cells"]]},{"id":38068,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"What is the role of CD4 and CD8 in T cell receptor signalling?","o":["They act as TCR co-receptors in MHC interactions; full signalling needs further co-receptors","They are the antigen-binding part of the TCR itself, replacing the α and β chains in mature T cells","They are soluble cytokines released after TCR binding","They degrade peptides before presentation","They form the peptide-binding groove of MHC"],"e":"CD4 and CD8 have an important role: they act as the TCR co-receptors in MHC interactions (CD4 with class II, CD8 with class I). However, full signal transduction inside a T cell involves additional co-receptor (adapter) molecules, covered in the year 2 Immune System module.","w":["The α and β chains form the antigen-binding domain.","CD4 and CD8 are surface molecules, not secreted cytokines.","Peptide degradation is done by the proteasome or endosomes.","The groove is part of the MHC molecule."],"k":"CD4/CD8 are TCR co-receptors; more co-receptors complete signalling.","rn":[["","Do CD4 and CD8 alone give the full TCR signal inside the T cell?","Do CD4 and CD8 alone give the full TCR signal inside the T cell?","No; Additional co-receptor molecules are needed"]]},{"id":38069,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"In this figure, which label shows the β chain gene segments in the immature T cell?","o":["3","2","5","6","7"],"e":"The labels show: 1 DNA of an immature T cell; 2 α chain segments Vα, Jα, Cα; 3 β chain segments Vβ, Dβ, Jβ, Cβ; 4 the immature T cell; 5 mRNA after rearrangement, with one V, (D), J and C joined for each chain; 6 the TCR made of α and β chains; 7 the mature T cell.","w":["2 is the α chain segments (no D).","5 is the rearranged mRNA.","6 is the assembled TCR.","7 is the mature T cell."],"k":"β chain has V, D, J and C segments; α has V, J and C.","f":"tc14","rn":[["","CLIP","CLIP","Class II-associated invariant chain peptide"],["","How is TCR diversity achieved?","How is TCR diversity achieved?","Gene recombination; Random segment combining"],["","Alpha vs beta TCR subunit: gene segments","Alpha vs beta TCR subunit: gene segments","Alpha: V, J, C; Beta: V, D, J, C"]]},{"id":38070,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"In the thymus, a developing T cell's receptor cannot bind any peptide. What happens to it, and by what process?","o":["It is deleted by apoptosis in positive selection","It is deleted by apoptosis in negative selection","It is kept as a memory T cell for later use","It is converted into a B cell in the bone marrow","It undergoes affinity maturation until it binds"],"e":"T cells that express receptors incapable of binding peptides would be effectively useless, and they are deleted by apoptosis in the thymus by positive selection. Negative selection deletes T cells whose TCR binds too strongly to self-MHC.","w":["Negative selection removes T cells that bind self-MHC too strongly.","Useless T cells are deleted, not kept.","T cells do not become B cells.","T cells do not undergo affinity maturation."],"k":"Positive selection deletes T cells that cannot bind peptide.","rn":[["","Positive selection","Positive selection","TCR cannot bind peptide; Deleted by apoptosis; In thymus"]]},{"id":38071,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"A developing T cell's receptor binds very strongly to self-MHC. Why is it deleted?","o":["It would pose a risk of causing autoimmunity","It would be unable to bind any peptide","It would secrete too much antibody against foreign antigens after release","It would block the proteasome","It would switch class to IgE"],"e":"In negative selection, any T cell whose TCR binds too strongly to self-MHC molecules is deleted, because it would pose a risk of causing autoimmunity. Positive selection removes T cells that cannot bind peptides at all.","w":["Inability to bind peptide is the target of positive selection.","T cells do not secrete antibody.","The proteasome is not affected by T cells.","T cells have no antibody classes."],"k":"Negative selection removes strongly self-reactive T cells.","rn":[["","Why is a T cell whose TCR binds self-MHC too strongly deleted?","Why is a T cell whose TCR binds self-MHC too strongly deleted?","Risk of causing autoimmunity"]]},{"id":38072,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","a":["negative selection"],"s":"Name the thymic process that deletes T cells whose receptors bind too strongly to self-MHC.","e":"Negative selection deletes T cells whose TCR binds too strongly to self-MHC molecules, as they would pose a risk of causing autoimmunity.","k":"Negative selection prevents autoimmunity.","rn":[["","Negative selection","Negative selection","TCR binds self-MHC too strongly; Deleted in thymus"]]},{"id":38073,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","a":["positive selection"],"s":"Name the thymic process that deletes T cells whose receptors cannot bind peptides.","e":"Positive selection: T cells expressing receptors incapable of binding peptides would be useless, so they are deleted by apoptosis in the thymus.","k":"Positive selection removes useless T cells.","rn":[["","Positive selection","Positive selection","TCR cannot bind peptide; Deleted by apoptosis; In thymus"]]},{"id":38074,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which statement correctly describes what each T cell type does on activation?","o":["CD4+ cells secrete chemical messengers; CD8+ cells trigger apoptosis and damage the cell membrane","CD4+ cells trigger apoptosis of target cells; CD8+ cells secrete chemical messengers that stimulate responses","Both secrete antibodies against the antigen","Both phagocytose the infected cell","CD4+ cells present antigen to CD8+ cells on MHC I"],"e":"On activation, CD4+ T cells secrete a variety of chemical messengers that stimulate immune responses. CD8+ T cells engage with and attack target cells by triggering apoptosis and damaging the cell membrane.","w":["This swaps the two activities.","T cells do not secrete antibodies.","Phagocytosis is not their mechanism.","T cells are not described as presenting antigen to each other."],"k":"CD4: messengers; CD8: apoptosis and membrane damage.","rn":[["","CD4+ vs CD8+: action on activation","CD4+ vs CD8+: action on activation","CD4+: secrete chemical messengers; CD8+: attack target cells"]]},{"id":38075,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which statement about full activation of CD4+ and CD8+ T cells is consistent with the table and the lecture?","o":["CD4+ needs an APC; CD8+ needs an infected target cell and often CD4+ help","CD4+ needs an infected target cell; CD8+ needs only an APC","Both are fully activated by soluble antigen alone, without needing any other cell to be involved","Neither needs any other cell to be activated","CD8+ cells always need B cell help"],"e":"The table says CD4+ T cells require stimulation by an APC before full activation, and CD8+ T cells require stimulation by an infected target cell AND a CD4+ helper T cell. The lecturer clarified that the \"AND\" is not every time: CD4 help is sometimes needed for a higher-magnitude response or full activation.","w":["This swaps the requirements.","T cells cannot recognise soluble antigen directly.","Both need presenting cells.","B cell help is not the requirement described."],"k":"CD4+: APC; CD8+: infected cell (+ CD4 help, not always).","rn":[["","What does a CD8+ T cell need for full activation?","What does a CD8+ T cell need for full activation?","Infected target cell; AND CD4+ helper T cell"]]},{"id":38076,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which T cell type typically responds to antigens derived from the exogenous pathway?","o":["CD4+ T cells","CD8+ T cells","Both equally","Neither; exogenous antigen is seen only by B cells","Only memory T cells"],"e":"The TCR of CD4+ T cells typically responds to antigens derived from the exogenous pathway (pathogen processed from outside, presented on MHC II). The TCR of CD8+ T cells responds to antigens from the endogenous pathway (pathogens in the cytoplasm, presented on MHC I).","w":["CD8+ cells respond to the endogenous pathway.","The pathways map to different T cells.","CD4+ T cells see exogenous antigen on MHC II.","Memory status is not the deciding factor."],"k":"Exogenous → CD4+; endogenous → CD8+.","rn":[["","Exogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","Exogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","CD4+ helper T cell"]]},{"id":38077,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which pairing of B cell and T cell features is correct?","o":["B cells recognise intact antigen; T cells recognise processed peptides presented by another cell","B cells need antigen to be presented on MHC first, while T cells recognise intact soluble antigen directly","Both recognise only processed peptides","Both recognise only intact antigen","B cells recognise only lipids; T cells only carbohydrates"],"e":"B cells recognise intact protein antigen, soluble or pathogen-associated (antibodies can bind antigen still on a bacterium's surface). T cells recognise peptides derived from processed antigen and presented to them by another cell.","w":["This swaps the two.","B cells recognise intact antigen.","T cells need processed, presented peptide.","Adaptive antigens are mainly protein; B cells only rarely see lipids or carbohydrates."],"k":"B: intact antigen; T: presented peptide.","rn":[["","B vs T cells: antigen recognised","B vs T cells: antigen recognised","B: intact protein; T: processed peptides; Presented by another cell"]]},{"id":38078,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which statement about the receptors of B and T cells is correct?","o":["The B cell receptor is on the surface and secreted; the TCR is on the surface only","Both receptors are secreted into the blood as soluble molecules","The TCR is secreted; the B cell receptor stays on the surface","Neither receptor is found on the cell surface","Both receptors undergo affinity maturation and later switch class during a secondary response"],"e":"The B cell receptor (antibody) is both on the cell surface and secreted in soluble form. The T cell receptor is on the cell surface only. B cells mutate their receptor during affinity maturation; T cells do not.","w":["The TCR is never secreted.","This reverses the two.","Both receptors are on the cell surface.","Only B cells undergo affinity maturation."],"k":"BCR: surface + secreted; TCR: surface only.","rn":[["","B cells vs T cells: receptor location","B cells vs T cells: receptor location","B: cell surface and secreted; T: cell surface only"]]},{"id":38079,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which statement about subsets of B and T cells is consistent with the lecture?","o":["T cells show major subset differentiation (CD4+ vs CD8+); B cell subsets show no major differences","B cells show major subsets; T cells are all identical","Both show four major subsets each","Neither has any subsets","B cell subsets differ in which MHC class they recognise, while T cells form one uniform pool"],"e":"Subsets of B cells are reported, but there are no major differences other than antigen specificity, so they are treated as one pool. T cells show major subset differentiation (CD4+ vs CD8+), which is why there are four classes of T cells.","w":["This reverses the two.","B cells do not have four major subsets.","T cells have major subsets.","B cells do not recognise MHC."],"k":"Major subsets: T cells (CD4 vs CD8), not B cells.","rn":[["","B vs T cells: subsets","B vs T cells: subsets","B: no major differences; T: CD4+ vs CD8+"]]},{"id":38080,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"An antibody binds amino acids that are far apart in a protein's sequence but brought close together by folding. Why could a T cell not recognise the same epitope?","o":["TCRs recognise only small linear peptides presented on MHC","TCRs bind only folded, three-dimensional epitopes","TCRs bind only lipid antigens, not proteins","TCRs recognise only carbohydrate side chains","TCRs need the antigen to stay attached to the pathogen's surface, so they cannot see processed fragments"],"e":"A B cell antibody can recognise a continuous epitope (adjacent amino acids in the primary structure) or a discontinuous epitope formed when the protein folds in 3D (called secondary structure in the notes). T cells cannot recognise such folded regions, because TCRs recognise only small linear peptides presented by MHC class I (CD8+) or class II (CD4+).","w":["TCRs cannot recognise folded epitopes.","T cell antigens are always protein peptides.","T cell antigens are protein peptides, not carbohydrates.","T cells need processed, presented peptides."],"k":"B cells see continuous and discontinuous epitopes; T cells see linear peptides only.","rn":[["","Why can't T cells see discontinuous epitopes?","Why can't T cells see discontinuous epitopes?","TCRs see small linear peptides; On MHC"]]},{"id":38081,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which statement defines a continuous epitope?","o":["A string of amino acids next to one another in the antigen's sequence","Amino acids far apart in sequence but close together after folding in three dimensions","A carbohydrate side chain attached to the protein during glycosylation","A lipid patch on the bacterial outer membrane","The constant region of an antibody molecule"],"e":"A continuous epitope is a string of amino acids immediately next to one another on the target antigen, part of the primary structure (the order of amino acids encoded in the gene). A discontinuous epitope is made of regions far apart in sequence that come close together when the protein folds.","w":["That describes a discontinuous epitope.","Epitopes in this context are amino acid regions.","A lipid patch is not the definition given.","The constant region is part of the antibody, not an epitope."],"k":"Continuous epitope = adjacent amino acids in primary structure.","rn":[["","Continuous epitope","Continuous epitope","String of adjacent amino acids; Part of primary structure"]]},{"id":38082,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","a":["discontinuous epitope","discontinuous","conformational epitope"],"s":"What term describes an epitope formed by amino acids that are far apart in the sequence but close together in the folded protein?","e":"A discontinuous epitope is formed when regions far apart in the primary sequence come close together in the folded protein. B cells can recognise these; T cells cannot, because TCRs recognise only small linear peptides on MHC.","k":"Discontinuous epitopes are recognised by B cells, not T cells.","rn":[["","Discontinuous epitope","Discontinuous epitope","Regions far apart in sequence; Brought close by protein folding"]]},{"id":38083,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) with adaptive response receptors?","o":["PRRs bind a limited array of PAMPs; adaptive receptors bind a potentially infinite array of peptides","PRRs bind a potentially infinite array of peptides; adaptive receptors bind only PAMPs","Both are entirely germline encoded","Both have molecular memory","PRRs are found only on T cells, where they detect peptides presented by MHC molecules"],"e":"PRRs bind a limited array of pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs), common bits of bacteria or fungi. Adaptive response receptors bind a potentially infinite array of pathogen-associated peptides and can be very specific.","w":["This reverses them.","Only PRRs are entirely germline encoded.","Only adaptive receptors have molecular memory.","PRRs are on innate cells."],"k":"PRRs: limited PAMPs; adaptive: near-infinite peptides.","rn":[["","PRRs vs adaptive receptors: range bound","PRRs vs adaptive receptors: range bound","PRRs: limited array of PAMPs; Adaptive: potentially infinite peptides"]]},{"id":38084,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which type of molecule do adaptive response receptors recognise, as a general rule?","o":["Protein peptides, with B cells only rarely recognising carbohydrates or lipids","Mainly lipids from bacterial membranes, with protein peptides recognised only rarely","Mainly carbohydrates, as PRRs do","Only nucleic acids","Only whole bacteria"],"e":"Antigens for adaptive receptors are always protein peptides for T cells, and only rarely carbohydrates or lipids for B cells. PRRs respond to PAMPs that may be protein, carbohydrate or lipid based.","w":["Lipids are only rarely recognised, by B cells.","Carbohydrate-based PAMPs are recognised by PRRs; adaptive antigens are mainly peptides.","Nucleic acids are not the general rule given.","T cells see peptides, not whole bacteria."],"k":"Adaptive antigens are protein peptides (B cells rarely lipid/carbohydrate).","rn":[["","Which cells rarely recognise carbohydrates or lipids?","Which cells rarely recognise carbohydrates or lipids?","B cells (their antibodies)"]]},{"id":38085,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"Which feature belongs to PRRs but NOT to adaptive response receptors?","o":["Their genes are entirely germline encoded","They induce molecular memory","They improve or adapt during the immune response","Gene editing modifies the genome of the cells that carry them","They can bind a potentially infinite array of peptides"],"e":"PRR genes are entirely germline encoded, and PRRs show no molecular memory or ability to improve during a response. Adaptive receptors induce molecular memory, improve and adapt during the response, and arise from gene editing (recombination) that modifies the genome of adaptive immune cells.","w":["Memory belongs to adaptive receptors.","Adapting during the response is adaptive.","Gene editing applies to adaptive cells.","An infinite binding array is adaptive."],"k":"PRRs: germline encoded, no memory.","rn":[["","PRRs vs adaptive receptors: genes","PRRs vs adaptive receptors: genes","PRRs: entirely germ line encoded; Adaptive: gene editing modifies genome"]]},{"id":38086,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"A PRR on a macrophage meets the same bacterium for the second time. Which outcome fits the lecture?","o":["No faster or more specific response, because PRRs have no memory","A much faster response, because the PRR has adapted","The PRR gene has been edited during the first infection, so it now binds a new peptide more tightly","The PRR becomes a T cell receptor","The PRR now recognises only self antigens"],"e":"PRRs lack immunological memory: they cannot adapt or become more specific, and they recognise PAMPs, common molecular patterns of pathogens. Memory and fine-tuning are features of adaptive response receptors.","w":["PRRs do not adapt.","PRR genes are not edited.","PRRs do not become TCRs.","PRRs recognise PAMPs, not self antigens."],"k":"PRRs give the same response on repeat exposure.","rn":[["","Why is the adaptive second response bigger and faster?","Why is the adaptive second response bigger and faster?","Memory B cells; Specific antibodies; Memory T cells"],["","PRRs vs adaptive receptors: memory","PRRs vs adaptive receptors: memory","PRRs: none, can't adapt; Adaptive: molecular memory; Improve during response"]]},{"id":38087,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A patient lacks functional MHC class II. Which consequence is most likely?","o":["CD4+ helper T cells cannot be activated, so B cell antibody responses fail","CD8+ T cells can no longer recognise infected body cells","All nucleated cells stop presenting self peptides","Thymic positive selection of all T cells is unaffected and responses are normal","B cells can no longer recognise intact antigen"],"e":"Class II MHC on professional APCs presents exogenous antigen to CD4+ helper T cells. Without it, helper cells are not activated; and because helper T cell double-checking is needed for full B cell activation, the antibody arm of the response would also fail. CD8+ recognition uses class I.","w":["CD8+ cells use class I on infected cells.","Self-peptide display on all nucleated cells uses class I.","Helper cell responses would not be normal without class II.","B cells recognise intact antigen through their receptor without MHC."],"k":"No MHC II → no Th activation → poor antibody response.","rn":[["","What happens to B cells without helper T cell double checking?","What happens to B cells without helper T cell double checking?","Never fully activated; No antibody arm of response"],["","What are the 2 stages of helper T-B activation?","What are the 2 stages of helper T-B activation?","Th cell primed; B cell activated by Th"]]},{"id":38088,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"Which pairing of process and cell type is correct?","o":["Class switching: B cells only","Affinity maturation: T cells only","Soluble receptor secretion: T cells only","Gene rearrangement of receptors: B cells only","MHC class II presentation: all nucleated cells"],"e":"Class switching and affinity maturation occur in B cells, not T cells. Only B cells secrete their receptor (antibody). Both B and T cells rearrange their receptor genes. MHC class II presentation is limited to professional APCs.","w":["Affinity maturation is a B cell feature.","T cells never secrete their receptor.","Both B and T cells rearrange receptor genes.","Class II presentation is restricted to professional APCs."],"k":"Class switching and affinity maturation are B cell features.","rn":[["","Do TCR constant domains get switched?","Do TCR constant domains get switched?","No; No class switching"]]},{"id":38089,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A virus has evolved to destroy dendritic cells. Which link in immunity is most directly disrupted?","o":["Presentation of antigen to T cells, the bridge between innate and adaptive immunity","Production of all red blood cells","Lysis of bacteria by complement","Germline encoding of PRR genes","Secretion of IgE from mast cells and basophils in the infected tissue"],"e":"Dendritic cells are professional antigen presenting cells whose sole purpose is to present antigens to T cells. The lecturer described APCs, particularly dendritic cells, as a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity. Losing them would disrupt T cell activation.","w":["Red cells come from the erythroid progenitor.","Complement acts independently of dendritic cells.","PRR genes are germline encoded regardless.","Mast cells do not secrete IgE; IgE binds mast cells."],"k":"Dendritic cells link innate and adaptive immunity via antigen presentation.","rn":[["","Which cells are seen as a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity?","Which cells are seen as a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity?","Dendritic cells (APCs)"]]},{"id":38090,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Which statement summarises the main T cell subsets in this lecture?","o":["The major subsets are CD4+ helper and CD8+ cytotoxic T cells","The major subsets are suppressor and memory T cells, which are the best understood","The major subsets are B1 and B2 cells","The major subsets are NK and dendritic cells","There is only one T cell subset"],"e":"T cells are divided into four functional classes (cytotoxic, helper, suppressor and memory), but the lecture focuses on the two major subsets: CD4+ helper T cells and CD8+ cytotoxic T cells. Knowledge about memory T cells is still emerging.","w":["Suppressor and memory are classes, but not the major subsets focused on.","B cell subsets are not T cell subsets.","NK and dendritic cells are not T cells.","T cells have four classes."],"k":"Major T cell subsets: CD4+ helper and CD8+ cytotoxic.","rn":[["","B vs T cells: subsets","B vs T cells: subsets","B: no major differences; T: CD4+ vs CD8+"]]},{"id":38091,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"Which pairing of response time is correct?","o":["Innate: minutes to hours; adaptive: days","Innate: days; adaptive: minutes to hours","Both: minutes to hours","Both: several weeks","Innate: seconds; adaptive: years"],"e":"The comparison table gives innate immunity a response time of minutes/hours and adaptive immunity days. The lecturer explained that adaptive immunity needs signalling molecules and cells to mature, so it takes days, whereas innate defences such as resident macrophages act straight away after a cut.","w":["This reverses the timings.","Adaptive immunity takes days.","Neither is described as taking weeks.","These timings are not given in the table."],"k":"Innate: minutes–hours; adaptive: days.","rn":[["","Innate vs adaptive: response time","Innate vs adaptive: response time","Innate: minutes/hours; Adaptive: days"]]},{"id":38092,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"Which statement about self/non-self discrimination is consistent with the comparison table?","o":["Innate is perfect; adaptive is very good but occasional failures cause autoimmune disease","Adaptive is perfect and never fails; innate often attacks self tissue and is the main cause of autoimmunity","Both are perfect and never react against self","Neither can tell self from non-self at all","Innate fails often because PAMPs are found on human cells"],"e":"Innate self/non-self discrimination is perfect because there are no microbe-specific patterns in the host: PAMPs such as peptidoglycan or lipopolysaccharide are not found in the human body. Adaptive discrimination is very good thanks to negative selection, but occasional failures result in autoimmune disease.","w":["This reverses the two.","Adaptive discrimination occasionally fails.","Both do discriminate.","PAMPs are absent from human cells."],"k":"Innate: perfect; adaptive: very good, rare failure → autoimmunity.","rn":[["","Innate vs adaptive: self/nonself discrimination","Innate vs adaptive: self/nonself discrimination","Innate: perfect; Adaptive: very good"],["","Failure of adaptive self/nonself discrimination causes what?","Failure of adaptive self/nonself discrimination causes what?","Autoimmune disease"]]},{"id":38093,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"Which pairing of soluble components is correct?","o":["Innate: many antimicrobial peptides and proteins; adaptive: antibodies","Innate: antibodies made by plasma cells; adaptive: many antimicrobial peptides and proteins","Both: only antibodies","Innate: T cell receptors; adaptive: complement","Neither has soluble components"],"e":"The comparison table lists many antimicrobial peptides and proteins as the soluble components of blood or tissue fluids in innate immunity, and antibodies in adaptive immunity. The second table adds lysozyme, complement, acute phase proteins (e.g. C-reactive protein), interferon and other cytokines for innate immunity, and antibody and cytokines for adaptive.","w":["This reverses the two.","Innate immunity also has soluble components.","TCRs are not soluble, and complement is innate.","Both have soluble components."],"k":"Soluble: innate antimicrobial proteins; adaptive antibodies.","rn":[["","Innate vs adaptive: soluble components of blood/tissue","Innate vs adaptive: soluble components of blood/tissue","Innate: antimicrobial peptides and proteins; Adaptive: antibodies"]]},{"id":38094,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which cells are listed as the major cell types of adaptive immunity?","o":["T cells, B cells and antigen-presenting cells","Phagocytes, natural killer cells and dendritic cells","Neutrophils and eosinophils only","Red blood cells and platelets","Mast cells and basophils only"],"e":"The comparison table lists T cells, B cells and antigen-presenting cells as major adaptive cell types. Innate cell types are phagocytes (monocytes, macrophages, neutrophils), natural killer cells and dendritic cells; the lecturer added that granulocytes are also important in innate defence.","w":["These are listed as innate cell types.","Neutrophils and eosinophils are innate.","Red cells and platelets are not immune cell types here.","Mast cells and basophils are innate granulocytes."],"k":"Adaptive cells: T cells, B cells, APCs.","rn":[["","What are the 3 professional antigen presenting cells?","What are the 3 professional antigen presenting cells?","Dendritic cells; Macrophages; B cells"],["","APC","APC","Antigen Presenting Cell"],["","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Which cells can present in MHC class II?","Only professional APCs; Dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells"]]},{"id":38095,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"In the response-to-infection table, how do first and second contact compare?","o":["Innate: + and +; adaptive: + and +++","Innate: + and +++; adaptive: + and +","Both: + and +++","Both: + and +","Innate: +++ and +; adaptive: + and +"],"e":"The second comparison table shows innate immunity responding at the same level (+) on first and second contact, while adaptive immunity responds + on first contact and +++ on second contact. The lecturer explained the difference is immunological memory: memory B cells, specific antibodies and memory T cells.","w":["This swaps the two systems.","Innate immunity does not increase on second contact.","Adaptive immunity increases on second contact.","Innate immunity is unchanged, not reduced."],"k":"Second contact: innate unchanged; adaptive greatly enhanced.","rn":[["","Innate vs adaptive: first vs second contact","Innate vs adaptive: first vs second contact","Innate: same (+, +); Adaptive: + then +++"]]},{"id":38096,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"A patient has chickenpox once and is then resistant to it. Which feature from the table does this illustrate?","o":["Adaptive immunity: resistance improved by repeated contact","Innate immunity: broad specificity with no specific memory","Innate immunity: resistance not improved by repeated contact","Adaptive immunity: response time of minutes","Innate immunity: many antimicrobial peptides"],"e":"The second table lists adaptive immunity as having antigen specificity and specific memory, with resistance improved by repeated contact. Innate immunity has broad specificity, no specific memory, and resistance not improved by repeated contact.","w":["Broad specificity without memory would not explain lasting resistance.","Innate resistance does not improve with repeated contact.","Adaptive immunity takes days, not minutes.","Antimicrobial peptides do not explain specific resistance."],"k":"Adaptive memory improves resistance with repeated contact.","rn":[["","Innate vs adaptive: effect of repeated contact","Innate vs adaptive: effect of repeated contact","Innate: resistance not improved; Adaptive: resistance improved"]]},{"id":38097,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"In this comparison table, which soluble factors are listed under the innate immune system?","o":["Lysozyme, complement, acute phase proteins such as C-reactive protein, interferon and other cytokines","Antibody and cytokines","T and B lymphocytes circulating through blood and lymph to find their antigens","Phagocytes and innate lymphoid cells","Antigen specificity and specific memory"],"e":"The table lists innate soluble factors as lysozyme, complement, acute phase proteins (e.g. C-reactive protein), interferon and other cytokines; adaptive soluble factors as antibody and cytokines. Innate cells are phagocytes and innate lymphoid cells including NK cells; adaptive cells are T and B lymphocytes.","w":["Antibody and cytokines are the adaptive soluble factors.","T and B lymphocytes are adaptive cells.","These are innate cells, not soluble factors.","These are adaptive features, not soluble factors."],"k":"Innate soluble factors: lysozyme, complement, CRP, interferon, cytokines.","f":"tc19","rn":[["","Innate soluble factors: 2 more?","Innate soluble factors: 2 more?","Acute phase proteins (CRP); Interferon, other cytokines"]]},{"id":38098,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"Which example shows the innate and adaptive systems working together?","o":["Antibody Fc domains activating the complement cascade","PRRs being entirely germline encoded in every innate immune cell of the body","T cells maturing in the thymus","Negative selection deleting self-reactive T cells","TCRs remaining membrane-bound"],"e":"The innate and adaptive systems do communicate. Examples on the slide: antibody (adaptive) Fc domains can activate the complement (innate) cascade; phagocytes (innate) can process and present antigen on MHC class II to T cells (adaptive); and NK cells (innate) have receptors that bind the Fc domain of antibodies, arming them to attack a specific target.","w":["This describes PRR genetics, not cooperation.","Thymic maturation is within adaptive immunity.","Negative selection is within adaptive immunity.","This is a TCR property, not cross-talk."],"k":"Cross-talk examples: Fc → complement; phagocyte MHC II presentation; NK–Fc binding.","rn":[["","3 examples of innate-adaptive communication?","3 examples of innate-adaptive communication?","Antibody Fc activates complement; Phagocytes present on MHC II; NK cells bind antibody Fc"],["","Which antibody class activates the complement cascade especially well?","Which antibody class activates the complement cascade especially well?","IgM (immunoglobulin M)"]]},{"id":38099,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"Which antibody class is best at activating the complement cascade, as the lecturer reminded the class?","o":["IgM","IgG","IgA","IgD","IgE"],"e":"During the lecture the class was asked which antibody class is most successful in activating the complement cascade. The answer was IgM, which works really well with the complement cascade. Complement activation by the antibody constant (Fc) domain is an example of adaptive–innate communication.","w":["IgG interacts with complement but less than IgM.","IgA does not interact strongly with complement.","IgD is a lymphocyte-bound receptor.","IgE binds mast cells and basophils."],"k":"IgM is the best complement activator.","rn":[["","Which antibody class activates the complement cascade especially well?","Which antibody class activates the complement cascade especially well?","IgM (immunoglobulin M)"]]},{"id":38100,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"A tumour cell is coated with antibodies, and a natural killer cell then attacks it. How does the NK cell recognise the target?","o":["Its receptors bind the Fc domain of the antibodies on the tumour cell","Its T cell receptor binds the tumour peptide presented on MHC class I by the tumour cell","It phagocytoses the antibodies first","Its PRRs bind the antibody variable regions","It secretes antibody that binds the tumour"],"e":"Natural killer cells possess receptors that bind the Fc domain of antibodies, arming themselves to attack a specific antigen target. The lecturer described antibodies sticking to a tumour cell, which then lets the NK cell bind via receptor–ligand interaction to the constant domain.","w":["NK cells do not use T cell receptors.","Phagocytosis is not the mechanism described.","NK receptors bind the Fc (constant) domain.","NK cells do not secrete antibody."],"k":"NK cells bind antibody Fc to attack antibody-coated targets.","rn":[["","How can NK cells attack tumours via antibodies?","How can NK cells attack tumours via antibodies?","Antibodies coat tumour; NK binds Fc domain; Arms NK against target"],["","How do NK cells arm themselves against a specific target?","How do NK cells arm themselves against a specific target?","Receptors bind antibody Fc domain"]]},{"id":38101,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"Which cell type did the lecturer describe as a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity?","o":["Dendritic cells","Erythrocytes","Plasma cells","Cytotoxic T cells","Platelets"],"e":"The lecturer said cytokines play a major role in communication, and antigen presenting cells, particularly dendritic cells, are considered a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity. Phagocytes processing and presenting antigen on MHC class II is another bridge.","w":["Erythrocytes carry oxygen.","Plasma cells secrete antibody.","Cytotoxic T cells are adaptive effector cells.","Platelets are not described as a bridge."],"k":"Dendritic cells bridge innate and adaptive immunity.","rn":[["","Which cells are seen as a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity?","Which cells are seen as a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity?","Dendritic cells (APCs)"]]},{"id":38102,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"Which statement about immunological memory provided by T cells is consistent with the lecture?","o":["It is an emerging field; much less is known than about B cell memory","It is fully understood and identical to B cell memory","T cells have no memory function at all, so all immune memory comes from B cells and antibody","T cell memory depends on class switching","T cell memory is stored in antibodies"],"e":"The lecturer explained that B cell memory development is well understood, but memory induced by T cells is still an emerging field and not much is known. Since COVID-19, it has been in the spotlight for researchers, and new discoveries are emerging all the time. Memory T cells persist semi-dormant for life and are rapidly reactivated.","w":["T cell memory is less well understood.","Memory T cells are one of the four classes.","T cells do not class switch.","Antibodies are B cell products."],"k":"T cell memory is an emerging, less understood field.","rn":[["","How well understood is T cell memory?","How well understood is T cell memory?","Emerging field; Less than B cell memory"]]},{"id":38103,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"Which event brought T cell immunological memory into the research spotlight, according to the lecturer?","o":["The COVID-19 pandemic","The discovery of penicillin","The first heart transplant","The invention of the light microscope","The eradication of smallpox"],"e":"The lecturer said that since COVID-19, T cell memory came into the spotlight of researchers and scientists, with more work and discoveries emerging every day, specifically on immunological memory from the T cell component.","w":["Penicillin is not mentioned in this context.","Transplantation is not linked to this.","The light microscope is not mentioned.","Smallpox eradication is not mentioned."],"k":"COVID-19 spotlighted T cell memory research."},{"id":38104,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A transplanted organ is matched poorly at HLA-A, -B and -C. Which immune risk does this raise?","o":["Recipient T cells may not tolerate the donor class I complexes","The organ will lack MHC class II entirely","The recipient's B cells will stop making any antibody against the donor organ or pathogens","The recipient's PRRs will be edited","Thymic negative selection will restart in the donor organ"],"e":"Class I MHC on every cell presents self peptides, and T cells must not react to these. Because HLA-B, -C and -A encode class I, donors are matched as closely as possible in these regions. A poor match means recipient T cells may treat donor class I complexes as foreign.","w":["The organ still expresses MHC; class II is on its APCs.","B cell antibody production is not stopped by mismatch.","PRR genes are germline encoded and not edited.","Negative selection happens in the recipient's thymus, not the organ."],"k":"Class I HLA mismatch → T cells may react to donor cells.","rn":[["","Why is HLA important in donor matching?","Why is HLA important in donor matching?","Class I presents self peptides; T cells must not react"]]},{"id":38105,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"A child has an inherited defect that deletes T cells that fail positive selection AND also all T cells that pass it. Which outcome follows?","o":["No mature T cells leave the thymus, causing failure of T cell immunity","Only autoreactive T cells survive and leave the thymus to attack self tissues","B cells take over all T cell functions","Innate immunity gains memory","CD8 cells change into CD4 cells"],"e":"Positive selection removes useless T cells that cannot bind peptide, and negative selection removes T cells that bind self-MHC too strongly. If all T cells are deleted, none mature in the thymus, so T cell functions (helper coordination, cytotoxic killing) fail. B cells also need helper T cells for full activation.","w":["If all are deleted, none survive, autoreactive or not.","B cells cannot replace T cell functions.","Innate immunity does not gain memory.","CD8 cells do not convert into CD4 cells."],"k":"Thymic deletion of all T cells abolishes T cell immunity.","rn":[["","Why does adaptive immunity take days?","Why does adaptive immunity take days?","Needs signalling molecules; Cells need to mature"],["","Where do T cell progenitors migrate to differentiate and mature?","Where do T cell progenitors migrate to differentiate and mature?","Thymus"],["","Why is a T cell whose TCR binds self-MHC too strongly deleted?","Why is a T cell whose TCR binds self-MHC too strongly deleted?","Risk of causing autoimmunity"]]},{"id":38106,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"HIV infects CD4+ cells. Based on this lecture, which immune function would you expect to decline as CD4+ cells are lost?","o":["Helper T cell coordination, including full activation of B cells","Thymic negative selection of developing B cells before they leave the bone marrow","Innate recognition of PAMPs by PRRs","Production of red blood cells","Self/non-self discrimination by innate cells"],"e":"CD4+ T cells are helper cells that secrete cytokines to stimulate B cells and cytotoxic T cells and coordinate the adaptive response. Without the helper double-check, B cells never become fully activated, and CD8+ cells may lack help for full activation. Innate PRR recognition does not depend on CD4+ cells.","w":["B cells are not selected in the thymus.","PRR recognition is innate and independent of CD4+ cells.","Red cell production is not a CD4+ function.","Innate discrimination relies on PAMPs, not CD4+ cells."],"k":"Loss of CD4+ cells removes helper coordination and B cell help.","rn":[["","What does a CD8+ T cell need for full activation?","What does a CD8+ T cell need for full activation?","Infected target cell; AND CD4+ helper T cell"]]},{"id":38107,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A drug blocks CD8 so it can no longer double-check MHC class I. What would most likely happen to cytotoxic T cell killing of virus-infected cells?","o":["Killing would be reduced, because CTLs only kill once the CD8 check is complete","Killing would increase, because the CTLs would no longer need any antigen to recognise their targets","CTLs would switch to producing antibody","CTLs would begin presenting antigen to B cells","There would be no effect at all"],"e":"The CD8 molecule on the CTL simultaneously double-checks that the peptide is presented correctly, recognising that the MHC class I molecule is from a matched allele. The CTL will only kill the target cell once these engagements are complete, so blocking CD8 would reduce killing.","w":["CTLs still need antigen and the CD8 check.","CTLs do not make antibody.","CTLs do not present antigen to B cells.","Blocking the co-receptor would have an effect."],"k":"CTL killing requires the CD8 double-check.","rn":[["","When will the CTL kill the target?","When will the CTL kill the target?","Once all engagements complete"]]},{"id":38108,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A cancer vaccine is designed to make dendritic cells present tumour antigens taken up from outside on MHC class I. Which pathway does it exploit?","o":["The crossover pathway","Negative selection","Affinity maturation","Class switching","The exogenous pathway to MHC class II only"],"e":"The crossover pathway lets professional APCs take antigens in through the exogenous route and transfer the peptides to the endogenous route for MHC class I presentation, activating CD8+ cytotoxic T cells. It is an important component of cancer immunotherapy and vaccination strategies.","w":["Negative selection deletes self-reactive T cells.","Affinity maturation is a B cell process.","Class switching is a B cell process.","Standard exogenous presentation uses class II, not class I."],"k":"Cancer vaccines can exploit the crossover pathway.","rn":[["","The crossover pathway is used in which 2 strategies?","The crossover pathway is used in which 2 strategies?","Cancer immunotherapy; Vaccination"]]},{"id":38109,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A patient's B cells bind a bacterial toxin strongly, but no antibody is secreted. Helper T cells are absent. What best explains the result?","o":["B cells need helper T cell signals as well as antigen binding to be fully activated","B cells cannot bind any antigen without first presenting it on MHC class I to themselves","The toxin must first be presented on class I to B cells","B cells need CD8 to secrete antibody","B cells only secrete antibody in the thymus"],"e":"Antigen binding alone is not sufficient: B cells also need growth factors and the double-checking interaction with helper T cells (including CD40L–CD40 and secreted soluble factors). Without it, B cells never become fully activated, so the antibody response does not take place.","w":["B cells bind intact antigen with their receptor without MHC.","B cells recognise intact antigen; presentation is for T cells.","CD8 is the cytotoxic T cell co-receptor.","B cells are not activated in the thymus."],"k":"B cell antibody secretion needs helper T cell signals.","rn":[["","What happens to B cells without helper T cell double checking?","What happens to B cells without helper T cell double checking?","Never fully activated; No antibody arm of response"],["","Is antigen binding enough to activate a B cell?","Is antigen binding enough to activate a B cell?","No; Needs helper T help; And growth factors"]]},{"id":38110,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"Why might a person still get flu each winter yet recover quickly from a second infection with exactly the same strain? (Use the innate vs adaptive comparison.)","o":["Adaptive memory gives a faster, larger second response to the same antigen","Innate immunity improves with each repeated contact, so the second flu infection is cleared by phagocytes alone","PRRs gain specificity after the first infection","T cell receptors undergo affinity maturation","Antibodies are germline encoded for every strain"],"e":"Adaptive immunity has persistent memory with a faster response of greater magnitude on subsequent infection, and resistance improves with repeated contact. Innate immunity responds the same each time. A different strain would present different antigens, but the same strain meets existing memory.","w":["Innate resistance does not improve with repeated contact.","PRRs do not gain specificity.","T cells do not undergo affinity maturation.","Antibody diversity comes from rearrangement, not germline encoding of every strain."],"k":"Same antigen again → faster, larger adaptive response.","rn":[["","Why is the adaptive second response bigger and faster?","Why is the adaptive second response bigger and faster?","Memory B cells; Specific antibodies; Memory T cells"],["","Innate vs adaptive: memory responses","Innate vs adaptive: memory responses","Innate: none; Adaptive: persistent, faster, greater magnitude"]]},{"id":38111,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","a":["thymus","the thymus"],"s":"T cells emerge in the bone marrow. Name the organ where they develop and mature.","e":"T cells emerge in the bone marrow as progenitors and migrate to the thymus, where they differentiate and mature into helper and cytotoxic T cells, and where positive and negative selection take place.","k":"T cells mature in the thymus.","rn":[["","Where do T cell progenitors migrate to differentiate and mature?","Where do T cell progenitors migrate to differentiate and mature?","Thymus"],["","B vs T cells: site of development","B vs T cells: site of development","B: bone marrow; T: bone marrow, then thymus"]]},{"id":38112,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["mhc class ii","class ii","mhc ii","class 2","mhc class 2","mhc 2"],"s":"On which MHC class do CD4+ helper T cells recognise antigen?","e":"CD4+ T helper cells recognise antigens processed through the exogenous pathway and presented on MHC class II molecules on the membrane of antigen presenting cells.","k":"CD4+ ↔ MHC class II.","rn":[["","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","MHC class II"],["","CD4+ vs CD8+: MHC class recognised","CD4+ vs CD8+: MHC class recognised","CD4+: MHC class II; CD8+: MHC class I"],["","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","Captures via surface antibody; Processes (exogenous); Presents on MHC class II"]]},{"id":38113,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["mhc class i","class i","mhc i","class 1","mhc class 1","mhc 1"],"s":"On which MHC class do CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognise antigen?","e":"CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognise antigens processed through the endogenous pathway and presented on MHC class I molecules on the membrane of somatic cells.","k":"CD8+ ↔ MHC class I.","rn":[["","Endogenous pathway: which MHC class?","Endogenous pathway: which MHC class?","MHC class I"],["","CD4+ vs CD8+: MHC class recognised","CD4+ vs CD8+: MHC class recognised","CD4+: MHC class II; CD8+: MHC class I"],["","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","Exogenous pathway: which MHC class?","MHC class II"]]},{"id":38114,"t":"Antigen Presentation","a":["tap","transporter associated with antigen processing"],"s":"Name the transporter that moves cytosolic peptides into the rough ER in the endogenous pathway.","e":"TAP (transporter associated with antigen processing) moves peptides made by the proteasome into the rough ER, where they bind class I MHC.","k":"TAP: cytosol → RER peptide transport.","rn":[["","TAP","TAP","Transporter associated with antigen processing"]]},{"id":38115,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","a":["heterodimer","a heterodimer","alpha beta heterodimer"],"s":"The T cell receptor is made of an α and a β subunit. What is the term for a receptor made of two different subunits?","e":"T cell receptors include an alpha and a beta subunit, making the receptor a heterodimer (αβTCR).","k":"TCR = αβ heterodimer.","rn":[["","αβTCR","αβTCR","Heterodimer; Of alpha and beta subunits"]]},{"id":38116,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"Which statement best summarises how TCR diversity is achieved?","o":["Gene recombination similar to somatic recombination creates diverse variable domains","Affinity maturation fine-tunes the TCR variable domain after each infection, as in B cells","Class switching swaps the TCR constant domain","The germline encodes a separate TCR gene for every antigen","TCRs copy the variable regions of B cell antibodies"],"e":"Gene recombination events, similar to somatic recombination in B cells, achieve diversity of the T cell receptor variable domain: segments of the α (VJC) and β (VDJC) genes are randomly reshuffled. T cells do not undergo affinity maturation or class switching.","w":["T cells do not undergo affinity maturation.","T cells do not class switch.","There are too many antigen shapes for the germline to encode separately.","TCRs are made from their own gene segments."],"k":"TCR diversity: gene recombination of variable domains.","rn":[["","How is TCR diversity achieved?","How is TCR diversity achieved?","Gene recombination; Random segment combining"],["","What are the 2 domains of the TCR?","What are the 2 domains of the TCR?","Variable antigen binding domain; Membrane associated constant domain"],["","Innate vs adaptive: diversity","Innate vs adaptive: diversity","Innate: limited germ line receptors; Adaptive: highly diverse (recombination)"]]},{"id":38117,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"A virus switches off MHC class I expression in the cells it infects. Which arm of T cell immunity is it evading?","o":["Recognition and killing by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells","Recognition by CD4+ helper T cells of viral peptides presented on MHC class II","Antibody binding by B cells","Thymic positive selection","Complement activation by IgM"],"e":"CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognise antigens from the endogenous pathway presented on MHC class I on infected body cells, and only kill once the TCR and CD8 checks are complete. Without class I, infected cells are not recognised by CTLs. CD4+ cells use class II on APCs.","w":["CD4+ recognition uses class II on APCs.","B cells bind intact antigen without MHC.","Thymic selection happens during development, not infection.","Complement activation does not require MHC I."],"k":"Loss of MHC I hides infected cells from CTLs.","rn":[["","A CD8+ cell is likely to be which T cell?","A CD8+ cell is likely to be which T cell?","Cytotoxic T cell"],["","Endogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","Endogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","CD8+ cytotoxic T cell"],["","How do CTLs stop viral replication?","How do CTLs stop viral replication?","Kill the infected cell; Hostile for the virus"]]},{"id":38118,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A bacterium survives entirely outside host cells in tissue fluid. Which T cell pathway is most relevant to an adaptive response against it?","o":["Exogenous presentation on MHC class II to CD4+ helper T cells","Endogenous presentation on MHC class I to CD8+ T cells","Direct recognition of the intact bacterium by T cell receptors","PRR recognition by T cells","Crossover presentation in every epithelial cell"],"e":"The exogenous pathway evolved to deal with extracellular infections: professional APCs phagocytose the bacteria, process them and present peptides on MHC class II to CD4+ helper T cells, which then secrete messengers that drive B cell antibody production and other responses.","w":["The endogenous pathway handles intracellular infections.","TCRs cannot recognise intact bacteria.","PRRs are on innate cells, not T cells.","Crossover presentation occurs in professional APCs, not every epithelial cell."],"k":"Extracellular bacteria → exogenous/MHC II → CD4+ help.","rn":[["","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","Captures via surface antibody; Processes (exogenous); Presents on MHC class II"]]},{"id":38119,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"Which statement about the four T cell classes is NOT correct?","o":["Memory T cells secrete cytokines that start every new response","Cytotoxic T cells destroy target cells bearing non-self antigen on MHC I","Helper T cells secrete cytokines that stimulate B cells and cytotoxic T cells","Suppressor T cells terminate immune responses that are no longer needed","Memory T cells persist semi-dormant and are reactivated quickly"],"e":"Memory T cells persist for life in a semi-dormant state and are rapidly reactivated on second exposure, bypassing the primary response. It is helper T cells that secrete cytokines stimulating B cells and cytotoxic T cells. Cytotoxic T cells kill; suppressor T cells terminate responses.","w":["This correctly describes cytotoxic T cells.","This correctly describes helper T cells.","This correctly describes suppressor T cells.","This correctly describes memory T cells."],"k":"Cytokine secretion to drive responses is the helper T cell role.","rn":[["","What do helper T cells secrete?","What do helper T cells secrete?","Cytokines"],["","Does an activated B cell secrete cytokines?","Does an activated B cell secrete cytokines?","No; Only the helper T cell"]]},{"id":38120,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A phagocyte engulfs bacteria and then activates a helper T cell. Which statement best explains why this is an example of innate–adaptive communication?","o":["An innate phagocyte presents processed antigen on MHC II to an adaptive T cell","An adaptive B cell presents the bacterial antigen on MHC I to an innate natural killer cell","Complement activates the T cell receptor directly","The helper T cell becomes a phagocyte","PRRs are transferred from the phagocyte to the T cell"],"e":"Phagocytes are components of innate immunity, but through the exogenous pathway they can process antigen and present it on MHC class II to helper T cells of adaptive immunity, acting as a bridge between the two systems.","w":["B cells present on MHC II to T cells, not MHC I to NK cells.","Complement does not activate TCRs directly.","T cells do not become phagocytes.","PRRs are not transferred between cells."],"k":"Phagocyte MHC II presentation links innate and adaptive immunity.","rn":[["","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","How does a B cell present antigen to a helper T cell?","Captures via surface antibody; Processes (exogenous); Presents on MHC class II"],["","3 examples of innate-adaptive communication?","3 examples of innate-adaptive communication?","Antibody Fc activates complement; Phagocytes present on MHC II; NK cells bind antibody Fc"]]},{"id":38121,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A 25-year-old has a respiratory virus that has entered and is replicating inside her airway epithelial cells. Viral proteins are being made in the cytoplasm of these cells. Her immune system needs to kill the infected epithelial cells without harming uninfected neighbours.<br><br>How will the viral peptides be displayed, and which T cell will respond?","o":["Endogenous pathway on MHC class I, recognised by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells","Exogenous pathway on MHC class II, recognised by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells","Exogenous pathway on MHC class II, recognised by CD4+ helper T cells after phagocytosis","Endogenous pathway on MHC class I, recognised by CD4+ helper T cells","No presentation; the T cell receptor binds the free virus in the airway directly"],"e":"Step 1: viral proteins made in the cytoplasm are intracellular (endogenous) antigens; they are degraded by the proteasome, moved into the rough ER by TAP, loaded onto MHC class I and taken via the Golgi to the cell surface. Step 2: MHC class I presents to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, which kill the presenting infected cell. Epithelial cells are somatic cells, not professional APCs, so they do not use class II.","w":["CD8+ cells recognise MHC class I, not class II.","Epithelial cells are not professional APCs, and the virus is inside the cell.","MHC class I presents to CD8+, not CD4+, T cells.","TCRs cannot recognise soluble or pathogen-attached antigen."],"k":"Cytosolic viral protein → MHC I → CD8+ CTL.","rn":[["","Endogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","Endogenous pathway: which T cell responds?","CD8+ cytotoxic T cell"],["","Endogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Endogenous pathway: last 3 steps?","Rough ER: bind MHC I; Via Golgi to surface; Shown to CD8+ T cells"],["","Endogenous pathway: which MHC class?","Endogenous pathway: which MHC class?","MHC class I"]]},{"id":38122,"t":"The T Cell Receptor","s":"In a research model, immature T cells in the thymus are tested. Group A have TCRs that cannot bind any peptide. Group B have TCRs that bind very strongly to self-MHC. Group C have TCRs that bind peptide on MHC at a moderate level.<br><br>Which groups are deleted, and why?","o":["A (useless) by positive selection and B (autoimmunity risk) by negative selection","A by negative selection and B by positive selection","Only C, because moderate binding is unsafe","None, because T cell selection happens in the spleen","All three, because T cells are selected and mature only in the bone marrow, never the thymus"],"e":"Step 1: positive selection deletes (by apoptosis) T cells whose TCRs cannot bind peptides, as they would be effectively useless (group A). Step 2: negative selection deletes T cells whose TCRs bind too strongly to self-MHC, as they risk causing autoimmunity (group B). Group C survives.","w":["The two processes are swapped.","Moderate binding cells survive.","T cell selection happens in the thymus.","T cells develop and mature in the thymus."],"k":"Positive selection removes non-binders; negative removes strong self-binders.","rn":[["","Negative selection","Negative selection","TCR binds self-MHC too strongly; Deleted in thymus"],["","Positive selection","Positive selection","TCR cannot bind peptide; Deleted by apoptosis; In thymus"],["","Why is a T cell whose TCR binds self-MHC too strongly deleted?","Why is a T cell whose TCR binds self-MHC too strongly deleted?","Risk of causing autoimmunity"]]},{"id":38123,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A patient's B cells capture a protein antigen with their surface antibody and present processed peptide on MHC class II. However, a defect means the CD40 ligand on his helper T cells cannot bind CD40 on the B cells. His B cells bind antigen normally.<br><br>What is the most likely consequence?","o":["B cells are not fully activated, so the antibody response fails","B cells release cytokines to activate themselves instead","Cytotoxic T cells take over antibody production","B cells present the antigen on MHC class I to CD8+ T cells instead","Antibody production increases, because T cells are no longer needed"],"e":"Step 1: in stage 2 of the figure, CD40L on the primed helper T cell binds CD40 on the B cell to activate it; this double check confirms the MHC class II peptide is not a self peptide. Step 2: without this, B cells never become fully activated and the B cell/antibody arm does not happen; antigen binding alone is not enough. Only the T helper cell secretes cytokines.","w":["Only T helper cells secrete cytokines here.","Cytotoxic T cells do not make antibody.","B cells present captured antigen on MHC class II.","B cells need T cell help for full activation."],"k":"CD40L–CD40 help is needed for B cell activation.","rn":[["","What happens to B cells without helper T cell double checking?","What happens to B cells without helper T cell double checking?","Never fully activated; No antibody arm of response"]]},{"id":38124,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A vaccine researcher designs a vaccine so that dendritic cells take up viral protein from outside the cell but present peptides on MHC class I to CD8+ T cells. The dendritic cells do not themselves become infected.<br><br>Which pathway is being exploited, and why is it useful?","o":["The crossover pathway; APCs can present viral antigen on class I without being infected","The endogenous pathway; dendritic cells must first be infected themselves to present viral peptide on class I","The exogenous pathway; class II presents to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells","The pattern recognition pathway; PRRs present PAMPs to T cells","Affinity maturation; the TCR improves its binding over time"],"e":"Step 1: in professional APCs, antigen enters by the exogenous route, then the peptide fragments transfer to the endogenous route for MHC class I presentation: the crossover pathway. Step 2: this lets APCs take in and process viruses without risking becoming infected and destroyed themselves, and it is an important part of cancer immunotherapy and vaccination strategies.","w":["The crossover route avoids the need for infection.","Class II presents to CD4+ helper T cells.","PRRs do not present antigen to T cells.","T cells have no affinity maturation."],"k":"Crossover: exogenous uptake → MHC I presentation in APCs.","rn":[["","Why is the crossover pathway needed?","Why is the crossover pathway needed?","APCs process viruses; Without being infected"]]},{"id":38125,"t":"Antigen Presentation","s":"A 40-year-old needs a kidney transplant. Two donors are available. Donor 1 matches the recipient closely at the HLA-B, HLA-C and HLA-A regions. Donor 2 matches poorly at these regions but well elsewhere.<br><br>Why is donor 1 preferred?","o":["Class I MHC presents self peptides constantly, and T cells must not react to them","Class II MHC is found on every nucleated cell of the kidney, so it presents to CD8+ T cells constantly","HLA-B, C and A encode antibodies that cross the placenta","These regions encode the TCR α and β chains","Matching these regions prevents all innate immune responses"],"e":"Step 1: MHC proteins are encoded by HLA genes; the class I regions are B, C and A. Step 2: class I is expressed on the vast majority of nucleated cells and presents self (cytosolic) peptides constantly, and T cells must not react to them, so donors are matched at class I HLA regions B, C and A as closely as possible.","w":["Class II is on professional APCs; class I is on nearly all nucleated cells.","HLA genes encode MHC, not antibodies.","HLA genes encode MHC, not TCR chains.","Innate responses are not controlled by HLA matching."],"k":"Match class I HLA (B, C, A) to protect transplants from T cells.","rn":[["","Why is HLA important in donor matching?","Why is HLA important in donor matching?","Class I presents self peptides; T cells must not react"]]},{"id":38126,"t":"T Cell Types and Origin","s":"A pathologist examines a lymph node from a patient with a viral infection. On standard H&amp;E staining, the lymphocytes all look alike. After staining for surface proteins, one population is strongly positive for CD8 and another for CD4.<br><br>Which statement best interprets these findings?","o":["CD8+ cells are likely cytotoxic; CD4+ cells are likely helpers","CD8+ cells are B cells; CD4+ cells are NK cells","CD4+ cells lack CD8 entirely; CD8+ cells lack CD4","CD numbers describe each molecule's function","H&amp;E staining alone is enough to separate B and T cells by microanatomy"],"e":"Step 1: on H&amp;E it is virtually impossible to tell B and T lymphocytes apart, so they are identified by surface proteins (CD, cluster of differentiation). Step 2: CD8+ cells are likely cytotoxic T cells and CD4+ cells likely helper T cells. CD4+ does not mean no CD8, only that CD4 is more abundant; CD numbers reflect the order of discovery, not function.","w":["CD4 and CD8 mark T cell subsets.","CD4+ means CD4 is more abundant, not that CD8 is absent.","CD numbers show order of discovery, not function.","Lymphocytes cannot be told apart reliably on H&amp;E."],"k":"CD8 = cytotoxic; CD4 = helper; CD numbers = discovery order.","rn":[["","Does a CD4+ helper T cell lack CD8?","Does a CD4+ helper T cell lack CD8?","No; CD4 is more abundant; Co-receptor for its TCR"],["","A CD8+ cell is likely to be which T cell?","A CD8+ cell is likely to be which T cell?","Cytotoxic T cell"],["","What does a CD8+ T cell need for full activation?","What does a CD8+ T cell need for full activation?","Infected target cell; AND CD4+ helper T cell"]]},{"id":38127,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"Scientists compare a patient's B cells and T cells during a long infection. Over weeks, the B cells' antibodies bind the antigen more strongly, and the B cells switch from IgM to IgG. The T cells' receptors, which bind the same pathogen, do neither, and no soluble form of the TCR is ever found.<br><br>Which statement best explains the T cell findings?","o":["TCRs have no affinity maturation or class switching and are never soluble","TCRs mature in the spleen, which delays their improvement","TCRs bind only carbohydrate and lipid, not peptides","T cells switch class later, after the B cells have finished","TCRs are entirely germline encoded, like the pattern recognition receptors of innate cells"],"e":"Step 1: B cells mutate their receptor during affinity maturation and switch class (substitute C domains). Step 2: T cells have no affinity maturation, no substitution of C domains during the response (T cells have no classes to switch), and the TCR is never soluble: it always stays membrane-bound.","w":["T cells mature in the thymus.","TCRs recognise protein peptides.","T cells never class switch.","TCR diversity comes from gene rearrangement, not germline encoding."],"k":"TCR: no affinity maturation, no class switching, never soluble.","rn":[["","B cells vs T cells: affinity maturation","B cells vs T cells: affinity maturation","B: mutate receptor; T: none"],["","Do TCR constant domains get switched?","Do TCR constant domains get switched?","No; No class switching"],["","B vs T cell: can the receptor be soluble?","B vs T cell: can the receptor be soluble?","B: yes (antibody); T: never, membrane bound"]]},{"id":38128,"t":"Comparing T Cells and B Cells","s":"A protein antigen has two regions recognised by the immune system. Region 1 is a short string of adjacent amino acids. Region 2 is formed by amino acids far apart in the sequence that only come together when the protein folds. After the protein is processed and presented on MHC, only one region can be seen by T cells.<br><br>Which region can T cells recognise, and why?","o":["Region 1, because TCRs see only small linear peptides on MHC","Region 2, because TCRs bind the folded intact protein directly on the pathogen","Both, because TCRs and BCRs see identical epitopes","Neither, because T cells only recognise PAMPs","Region 2, because processing preserves folded structure"],"e":"Step 1: region 1 is a continuous epitope (adjacent amino acids) and region 2 a discontinuous epitope (brought together by folding). Step 2: B cells can recognise both, but T cells cannot recognise folded regions: TCRs recognise only small linear peptides presented by MHC class I (CD8+) or class II (CD4+).","w":["TCRs cannot recognise intact or folded antigen.","B cells see both; T cells see only linear peptides.","PAMPs are recognised by innate PRRs.","Presented peptides are small linear fragments."],"k":"T cells see only linear (continuous) peptides on MHC.","rn":[["","Why can't T cells see discontinuous epitopes?","Why can't T cells see discontinuous epitopes?","TCRs see small linear peptides; On MHC"]]},{"id":38129,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"A patient's tumour cells become coated with antibody. Natural killer (NK) cells then bind and kill these specific tumour cells, while ignoring uncoated cells. In another patient, macrophages that engulfed bacteria present bacterial peptides to helper T cells.<br><br>What do these two observations illustrate?","o":["Innate and adaptive immunity communicate through Fc receptors and MHC class II","Innate immunity has memory, which lets NK cells recognise tumour cells they have met before","Adaptive immunity works completely separately from innate cells","Macrophages switch class to produce antibody for the NK cells","NK cells present tumour peptides on MHC class I to helper T cells"],"e":"Step 1: NK cells have receptors that bind the Fc domain of antibodies, arming them to attack a specific antigen target. Step 2: phagocytes (innate) process antigen by the exogenous pathway and present it on MHC class II, bridging to adaptive immunity. Both show that innate and adaptive immunity communicate.","w":["Innate immunity has no memory; the specificity comes from antibody.","The two systems communicate, as these examples show.","Macrophages do not make antibody.","MHC class I presents to CD8+ cells, and NK cells are not described as presenting."],"k":"Innate–adaptive bridges: NK Fc receptors; phagocyte MHC II.","rn":[["","Are innate and adaptive immunity separate?","Are innate and adaptive immunity separate?","No, they communicate"],["","3 examples of innate-adaptive communication?","3 examples of innate-adaptive communication?","Antibody Fc activates complement; Phagocytes present on MHC II; NK cells bind antibody Fc"]]},{"id":38130,"t":"Innate vs Adaptive Immunity","s":"A child develops chickenpox and recovers after about 10 days. Years later, she is exposed again and develops no illness. Throughout, her neutrophils and macrophages responded in the same way on both occasions.<br><br>Which statement correctly compares her innate and adaptive responses?","o":["Innate responded equally both times; adaptive was faster and bigger the second time","Innate responded faster the second time thanks to memory neutrophils","Adaptive responded within minutes on first contact","Both responses were identical on first and second contact","Adaptive immunity failed, and innate immunity alone prevented the second illness"],"e":"Step 1: innate immunity responds in minutes/hours but the same on first and second contact (no specific memory; resistance not improved by repeated contact). Step 2: adaptive immunity takes days the first time, but on second contact is faster and of greater magnitude (+ vs +++) thanks to memory B cells, specific antibodies and memory T cells.","w":["Innate immunity has no memory.","Adaptive responses take days on first contact.","Adaptive immunity improves with repeated contact.","Protection on re-exposure comes from adaptive memory."],"k":"Innate: same each time; adaptive: +++ on second contact.","rn":[["","Why is the adaptive second response bigger and faster?","Why is the adaptive second response bigger and faster?","Memory B cells; Specific antibodies; Memory T cells"],["","Innate vs adaptive: response time","Innate vs adaptive: response time","Innate: minutes/hours; Adaptive: days"],["","Innate vs adaptive: memory responses","Innate vs adaptive: memory responses","Innate: none; Adaptive: persistent, faster, greater magnitude"]]}]);
